\par\par \b \uc2  Miyazawa - Cannot say if  \highlight22\uc2 Japan \highlight\uc2  to  \highlight22\uc2 intervene \highlight\uc2  on euro. \b0\par\par\uc2 123 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 November 2000\par 01:31\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2000 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, Nov 7 (Reuters) - Japanese Finance Minister Kiichi Miyazawa said on Tuesday he could not comment on whether \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  will \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support the euro. \par \par \uc2  Speaking to reporters as he left a cabinet meeting, Miyazawa said he was informed of the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's (ECB) recent \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  beforehand but \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  was not asked to join in. \par \par \uc2  The ECB \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  unilaterally in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets on Monday for the second trading day in a row, helping to prop up the beleaguered \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which was quoted around $0.8620 in early Tokyo trade on Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  One focus of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets is whether Japan may join in to help prop up the euro. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010822dwb702gyn\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
