\par\par \b \uc2  Asia  \highlight22\uc2 Forex \highlight\uc2 :  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\uc2  Fears Support Euro; Upside Heavy \b0\par\par\uc2  By Arran Scott \par \uc2  Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par \uc2 695 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 6 November 2000\par 06:31\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2000, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  TOKYO -(Dow Jones)- Concerns of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  put a floor under the euro in Asia Monday, as traders pondered whether Japanese authorities might pick up where the Europeans left off last week and try to boost the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  But sales pressure remained heavy on the upside, capping the European unit against the dollar. Monday afternoon, the euro was down slightly against the dollar although it managed to eke out a minor gain against the yen. \par \par \uc2  With the euro having slumped back after last Friday's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , some traders in Asia speculated that Japanese monetary authorities might take steps to prop up the euro against the yen. \par \par \uc2  In the event, however, that didn't happen, leaving \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  traders to wondering whether the ECB might make an appearance later in the global session. \par \par \uc2  Ryohei Muramatsu, treasury manager at Commerzbank, said that isn't likely. Nor is it really necessary, he said. \par \par \uc2  With the U.S. economy beginning to show clear signs of slowing down, the euro's time will soon come, Muramatsu said. \par \par \uc2  "The euro will begin to recover towards the end of this year and early next year," he said. \par \par \uc2  In the wake of Friday's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and on the eve of presidential elections in the U.S., \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  trading was generally subdued Monday. The euro changed hands between $0.8642-$0.8669. The dollar, meanwhile, remained between Y107.00-Y107.28. \par \par \uc2  At 0515 GMT, the euro was at $0.8659, lower than $0.8669 late Friday in New York. The euro was at $0.8598 in Singapore Friday. \par \par \uc2  Against the yen, the euro was quoted at Y92.82, up from Y92.74 in New York late Friday. The euro was at Y92.99 in Singapore late Friday. \par \par \uc2  The dollar was quoted at Y107.19, above Y106.98 late Friday in New York. The dollar was at Y108.15 late Friday in Singapore. Friday was a national holiday in Japan. \par \par \uc2  Japanese vice finance minister for international affairs Haruhiko Kuroda kept \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  traders on guard Monday, saying \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  would \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets as deemed necessary. \par \par \uc2  "The euro was too weak against both the yen and the dollar. It was natural that the ECB \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," Kuroda also said. \par \par \uc2  While Friday's action by the ECB has helped to put a limit on the euro's downside for now, traders said a move higher won't be easy. \par \par \uc2  In addition to a large amount of sales interest by players eager to take profits on long positions created lower down, traders bullish on the euro will also have to contend with a barrage of technical resistance levels on the upside. \par \par \uc2  The euro will likely encounter resistance at $0.8695, $0.8710, $0.8800 and $0.8860, said a senior dealer at a leading Japanese trust bank. \par \par \uc2  "Looking at it from a technical perspective, the euro is extremely heavy," he said. \par \par \uc2  Market participants said trading in the major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  will likely remain subdued until the elections in the U.S. are over. \par \par \uc2  "That said, however, the new stance on policy won't be immediately clear," the trust bank dealer said. \par \par \uc2  As a result, traders said it will take time before they can discern whether the eventual outcome of the presidential race will be positive or negative for the dollar. \par \par \uc2  Kenneth Landon, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Deutsche Bank in Tokyo, however, said a victory by George W. Bush would be "very bullish" for U.S. financial markets and the dollar. \par \par \uc2  "Provided, of course, that Bush appoints a hard-dollar Treasury Secretary," he said. \par \par \uc2  Monday afternoon in Tokyo, Sterling was at $1.4472, down from $1.4488 in New York. \par \par \uc2  -By Arran Scott, Dow Jones Newswires; 813-5255-2929; arran.scott@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2  The chart of late Tokyo \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates which accompanied Asia \b \highlight22\uc2 Forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been replaced with an enhanced \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  fixture which is updated hourly. \par \par \uc2  Subscribers can find this table: \par \par \uc2  -On Telerate pages 4046 and 1087. \par \par \uc2  -On Dow Jones Newswires by searching the code N/IFX \par \par \uc2  -On Reuters by entering FRX or INT \par \par \uc2  -On Bloomberg by entering NI FRX \par \par \uc2  1074 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010805dwb605d7o\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
