\par\par \b \uc2  ANALYSIS-ECB opts for trusty ploy of doggedness in  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  fight. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Swaha Pattanaik \par \uc2 898 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 6 November 2000\par 15:44\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2000 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, Nov 6 (Reuters) - The European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has chosen doggedness over shock tactics in its battle to bolster the euro - a ploy which has served industrial powers well in their past tussles with the foreign exchanges. \par \par \uc2  The element of surprise which the Group of Seven nations had on their side when they jointly \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the euro's support in September has all but disappeared after the ECB went on a solo euro-buying spree for the second consecutive trading day on Monday. \par \par \uc2  This means the ECB is getting a smaller bang for the bucks it is selling with each passing day. Matters are also not helped by the fact the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has recently \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  without U.S. or Japanese help, either by choice or necessity. \par \par \uc2  But history shows G7 members tend to get their way on exchange rates once they commit themselves to protracted trench warfare, even when they act alone, analysts and investors said. \par \par \uc2  "The ECB has effectively said it is putting a floor under the euro and that it will be there to support it, repeatedly if need be," said Adrian Cunningham, head of economics at Abbey National Asset Managers in Glasgow. \par \par \uc2  "It is having a smaller impact because they are acting without the Fed at their side but the ECB has a lot of cash in its reserves and it looks like it is willing to use it. All other things being equal, I would be more confident about holding euros." \par \par \uc2  Cunningham said his firm had a neutral position on the euro but added he was biased towards buying the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the months ahead. \par \par \uc2  SOLO BATTLES CAN SUCCEED \par \par \uc2  The return visit which the ECB paid the foreign exchanges on Monday got analysts drawing parallels with \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to curb exporting-crimping yen strength by selling its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  at least 17 times between June 1999 and April 2000. \par \par \uc2  Japan also acted on its own and offers a salutary lesson to those who think the ECB is doomed to fail because it is working alone - Japan's repeated offensives have trapped the yen in well-defined ranges against the dollar for more than a year now. \par \par \uc2  "The ECB has set out their stall and it looks like they mean it," said Nick Parsons, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Commerzbank. \par \par \uc2  "It will take some time as there are enough sceptics out there but over time it will work." \par \par \uc2  Moreover, persistence has been needed even when the G7 have turned their combined firepower on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, as was the case in 1994-1995 when they acted in the dollar's defence. \par \par \uc2  More than 10 central banks \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to buy dollars on at least four separate occasions between May 1994 and May 1995, with the United States, Japan, and Germany coordinating more dollar purchases in the months that followed. \par \par \uc2  Gloom about the dollar's outlook was as rife in 1995 when it was plumbing record lows against the mark as it has been more recently about the euro, which has lost as much as 30 percent of its value against the dollar since its January 1999 launch. \par \par \uc2  But the G7 \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  of 1994-95 laid the foundations for a rally which has since taken the dollar to its highest since the mid-1980s in trade-weighted terms. \par \par \uc2  While the ECB is unlikely to set its sights so high, it could easily get the euro back above $0.90 and hold it above there if it proves itself to be a persistent visitor to the foreign exchanges, analysts said. \par \par \uc2  And given the European system of central banks had \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves worth 276.8 billion euros ($239.5 billion) at the end of October, the ECB is unlikely to be strapped for cash anytime soon. \par \par \uc2  "They have finally decided that they need to show a strong hand to break the spirit of the speculators and have more than enough reserves to do it," said Neil Parker, treasury economist at Royal Bank of Scotland Financial Markets in London. \par \par \uc2  "In the long run, they will be successful." \par \par \uc2  ECB MAY NOT ALWAYS PLAY LONE HAND \par \par \uc2  Also, while the ECB has made its most recent forays into the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets on its own, analysts said it would be rash to jump to the conclusion that it was therefore waging a solitary battle to turn the euro around. \par \par \uc2  The ECB has said that its \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was in line with the G7's September statement which said the euro's weakness posed a potential threat to the world economy and that they would continue to monitor developments and cooperate as needed. \par \par \uc2  The potential for the U.S. to join in coordinated \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was expected to be back on the agenda once Tuesday's presidential elections were out of the way, particularly if Democratic nominee Al Gore won. \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile, Japan on Monday made it clear it was ready and willing to help engineer a stronger euro. \par \par \uc2  "There is a good chance of joint G7 \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  after the U.S. elections, certainly if Gore wins, and it would definitely be in Japan's interest," said Mike Lenhoff, portfolio strategist at Capel-Cure Sharp in London. \par \par \uc2  "If their objective is fairly limited and it is going with the grain of policy and economic fundamentals, it has a good chance of working. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010822dwb602o7e\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
