\par\par \b \uc2  Snapshot of euro performance as ECB meets - Oct 5. \b0\par\par\uc2 964 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 Oktober 2000\par 09:35\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2000 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, Oct 5 (Reuters) - The European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  (ECB) holds its regular policy-making meeting on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  Economists polled by Reuters expect the ECB to leave the minimum bid rate for its main refinancing tenders unchanged at 4.50 percent. It last hiked rates on August 31. \par \par \uc2  The following is a snapshot of the euro's performance to date and a selection of the most recent comments by ECB and other European officials on the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  EURO MOVES TO DATE: \par \par \uc2  ** The euro is one percent above the levels at which it closed against the dollar on September 14, the day of the ECB's previous policy meeting. It was trading around $0.8747 at 0650 GMT on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  ** The euro fell to a record low near $0.8440 on September 20, down 28 percent from levels at which it began trading after its January 1999 launch. \par \par \uc2  ** The euro rose by nearly 5.50 percent to peaks above $0.90 on September 22, up seven percent from record lows and its highest in nearly three weeks, after the Group of Seven conducted joint \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to defend the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  ** The euro fell to record lows near 90.00 yen on September 20, down more than 32 percent since its launch. It rallied by around 5.5 percent on September 22, following joint \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . It was trading around 95.40 yen on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  ** The euro fell to a record low of 56.73 pence against sterling on May 4, down nearly 22 percent from peaks it scaled on its first day of trading in January 1999. It rebounded by nearly 2.5 percent on September 22, following joint \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and was trading around 60 pence on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  ** The Bank of England's trade-weighted effective exchange rate index for the euro is down 18 percent since the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's launch. \par \par \uc2  LATEST COMMENTS ON THE EURO BY POLICYMAKERS \par \par \uc2  * French Finance Minister Laurent Fabius said on September 29 following a meeting of euro zone finance ministers: \par \par \uc2  "We are of the view there is still considerable headroom for the euro to appreciate." \par \par \uc2  * Greek Finance Minister Yannos Papandoniou said on September 29 of Denmark's rejection of the euro: \par \par \uc2  "The euro \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the euro zone will move ahead despite this unfortunate political event." \par \par \uc2  * ECB board member Eugenio Domingo Solans said in an interview with Agefi financial newspaper published on September 28: \par \par \uc2  "I believe that the euro is a success and is perceived as such by (European) citizens." \par \par \uc2  * ECB Vice President Christian Noyer said on September 27: \par \par \uc2  "We have an interest in a strong euro." \par \par \uc2  * Bundesbank President Ernst Welteke said in German magazine Stern on September 27 he was "certain that the euro's weakness will not continue". \par \par \uc2  In reference to further \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , he said: \par \par \uc2  "The war chest of European central banks is well-filled." \par \par \uc2  * ECB council member and Luxembourg \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  chief Yves Mersch said on September 26, in reference to further joint \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 : \par \par \uc2  "We have all the ammunition that is necessary if needed." \par \par \uc2  * Luxembourg Prime Minister and Finance Minister Jean-Claude Juncker said on September 26, in reference to G7 \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 : \par \par \uc2  "The message is very clear because Europe, like the U.S. and Japan, had the feeling it was useful and necessary to give the message to the market that we do want a strong euro." \par \par \uc2  "The markets must know that we will not accept that the sound economic fundamentals of Europe are not taken into account, so the message is clear." \par \par \uc2  * Italy's Treasury Minister Vincenzo Visco said in the aftermath of the joint Group of Seven \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 : \par \par \uc2  "We are now looking to the markets to continue the work." \par \par \uc2  * ECB President Wim Duisenberg said on September 23 that he agreed with International Monetary Fund chief economist Michael Mussa, who said on September 19 that the euro was "somewhat too low relative to fundamentals". \par \par \uc2  Speaking of the G7 \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  Duisenberg said: \par \par \uc2  "We all came to the conclusion that recent \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  movements had gone far beyond what was justified by fundamentals." \par \par \uc2  He said the misalignment between the major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  had gained too much momentum: \par \par \uc2  "We wanted to stop that and introduce an orderly reversal." \par \par \uc2  He said further possible \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  "will take place when we deem them appropriate". \par \par \uc2  "The U.S. thinks a strong dollar is in the interests of the U.S. and equally Europe thinks a strong euro is in the interest of Europe - and we can live together." \par \par \uc2  "The ECB does not pursue any \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  target. Our objective is the maintenance of stable prices in the euro zone and not a specific level of the exchange rate." \par \par \uc2  * French Finance Minister Laurent Fabius said on September 22 in reference to the G7 \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 : \par \par \uc2  "This \b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2  follows the position expressed by the finance ministers of the euro area in Versailles on September 8 and shared by the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  and I fully approve." \par \par \uc2  * The ECB said in a statement issued after the G7 \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 : \par \par \uc2  "On the initiative of the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , monetary authorities of the United States and \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  joined with the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  in concerted \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ...because of their shared concern about the potential implications of recent movements in the euro exchange rate." \par \par \uc2  * The ECB said in its monthly report for September published on September 21: \par \par \uc2  "The decline in the exchange rate of the euro contrasts more and more with economic fundamentals." \par \par \uc2  "Exchange rate developments appear to be increasingly out of line with global current account developments in the balance of payments." \par \par \uc2  "Against the backdrop of economic developments in the U.S., Japan and the euro area, standard economic reasoning seems increasingly insufficient to provide a convincing justification for the decline in the external value of the euro." \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010821dwa505lmx\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
