\par\par \b \uc2  BOJ Policy Board member Taya's speech - summary. \b0\par\par\uc2 317 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 September 2000\par 05:07\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2000 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  KUMAMOTO, Japan, Sept 28 (Reuters) - Following are key points from a speech by Bank of Japan (BOJ) Policy Board member Teizo Taya on Thursday to business leaders in this southern Japanese city. \par \par \uc2  - In the medium to long term, it may become necessary to set a target for a slight increase in prices. He said he wants to consider more specific ways to express numerically Japan's price movements. \par \par \uc2  - Persistent falls in Japan's consumer price index reflected positive factors such as competition and technological innovation, rather than weak demand. \par \par \uc2  - Impact from recent rises in oil prices on Japanese prices has been limited. \par \par \uc2  - Hopes last Friday's joint \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the United States, Europe and \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  will help to stabilise movements in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market. \par \par \uc2  - Japan's economy is on a recovery path, led by corporate capital spending, and personal consumption is heading toward to a gradual recovery. Corporate capital spending has been led by information technology-related investment, but is now beginning to spread through other sectors. \par \par \uc2  - Support from public works projects may decrease in the second half of this year, but private housing investment may not fall as much as people had expected at the beginning of the fiscal year to next March. \par \par \uc2  - Japan's exports are likely to continue to rise due to steady economic expansion overseas, although some impact from recent rises in the yen may appear. \par \par \uc2  - Japanese price movements have been stable. Recent falls in consumer prices were caused by technological innovation, deregulation, the rise in the yen and more international competition, not by weak demand. \par \par \uc2  - Financial markets have reacted calmly to the BOJ's decision in August to end its policy of guiding key short-term rates virtually to zero. \par \par \uc2  - The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has stressed it would continue to support the economy with an ultra-low interest rate policy after abandoning zero rates. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010821dw9s067ig\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
