\par\par \b \uc2  Euro relieved as Danish vote keeps doubters away. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Wayne Cole \par \uc2 899 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 September 2000\par 07:41\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2000 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, Sept 27 (Reuters) - The euro kept its tentatively firmer tone on Wednesday as the looming referendum in Denmark conjured up images of political uncertainty, market volatility and quite possibly \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  With opinion polls showing Thursday's vote on whether to join the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  too close to call and endless speculation about how the Group of Seven (G7) would respond, dealers were putting discretion ahead of valour and squaring short positions. \par \par \uc2  That helped the euro hold up at $0.8825 in late trade having risen almost a cent on Tuesday to $0.8826 in New York. \par \par \uc2  Traders reported some last gasp yen demand from Japanese investors ahead of the half-year bookclosing. Wednesday is the last day for spot \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  transactions since settlement will come Friday, the last business day of the fiscal half-year. \par \par \uc2  Against the yen it was at 94.85 yen from 94.96 in New York and well above last week's record lows around 90.05. \par \par \uc2  But dealers also remembered that when the previous fiscal year ended on Friday, March 31 it was marked by massive Japanese selling of euro/yen as investors hedged early for the new year. \par \par \uc2  That drove it deep under 100.00 yen for the first time and drew aggressive Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to restrain the yen on the following Monday. \par \par \uc2  Which left traders wondering if there would be a repeat performance this time or whether the G7's promise of support for the euro would be enough to forestall the hedgers. \par \par \uc2  The dollar, meanwhile, wended its way through another uneventful session against the yen to stand at 107.49 yen from 107.55 in New York. Japanese officials were keen to keep the threat of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  foremost in dealers' minds, with the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  declaring that the market was taking seriously the G7's rare concerted action. \par \par \uc2  He need not have bothered since market nerves are already stretched taut ahead of Denmark's decision on whether to join the euro, a vote which polls show is too close to call. \par \par \uc2  Dealers complained that positioning for the referendum was proving a real headache with many not sure how much it mattered. \par \par \uc2  Some observers, particularly eurozone officials, have played down the vote as just a domestic hiccup which does not mean anything for the success or otherwise of the euro experiment. \par \par \uc2  Others, mainly offshore, argue it would be a serious psychological blow in part since it would encourage Sweden and Britain to stay out. \par \par \uc2  And then there was the G7 to consider. \par \par \uc2  "There's talk they might \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  ahead of the vote, or maybe during or perhaps after," complained one European bank dealer. "Some say they may \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  even if it is a 'yes' vote, try and go with the momentum." \par \par \uc2  Given this uncertainty he reckoned that the balance of risks argued against going into the vote short of euros. \par \par \uc2  "Perhaps that's why the euro's up. People are squaring-up maybe even going a bit long on the hope that if it's a 'yes' the euro rises; if it's a 'no' the G7 \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the euro rises." \par \par \uc2  However, all dealers stressed that any rally was merely a short term speculative adjustment and not a revival of demand from real money managers, many of whom have been badly burned by the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's long slide. \par \par \uc2  Instead global fund managers had bought into the American dream with a vengeance. Data out from the U.S. Treasury this week showed European investors alone bought a massive $94 billion worth of U.S. equities in the first half of this year, while U.S. investors' buying of all foreign stock came to just $29 billion. \par \par \uc2  In all, net foreign investment in Wall Street amounted to $228 billion in the first half, which oddly enough is roughly equal to the entire \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves of the eurozone. \par \par \uc2  And that sum did not include the rash of European corporate bids for U.S. companies - analysts estimate around $160 billion worth are still in the pipeline. \par \par \uc2  "That's the tide the ECB has to stop, if not turn," said the European bank dealer. "But it's also the tide that fuels the U.S. trade deficit, which is why the market suspects Treasury are reluctant partners in \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ." \par \par \uc2  "They have to stabilise the euro and keep funds flowing into the States. A neat trick if you can do it." \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  bid prices at 0524 GMT. All data taken from Reuters \par \par \uc2  with percent change calculated from the daily U.S. close at 2130 \par \par \uc2  GMT. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \tab \tab \tab      Last     US Close  % Chg  YTD %   1999 Cls\par \tab \tab \tab \tab \tab   26 Sep.\par \tab \tab \tab      -----------------------------------------\par  Euro/dlr\tab \tab   0.8828   0.8826   +0.02   -12.33   1.0070\par  Dlr/yen\tab \tab    107.47   107.55   -0.07    +5.15   102.21\par  Euro/yen\tab \tab    94.88    94.96   -0.08    -7.97   103.10\par  Dlr/swiss\tab \tab  1.7244   1.7246   -0.01    +8.44   1.5902\par  Stg/dlr\tab \tab    1.4632   1.4634   -0.01    -9.55   1.6176\p