\par\par \b \uc2  Market takes G7  \highlight22\uc2 intervention \highlight\uc2  seriously -  \highlight22\uc2 Japan \highlight\uc2  MOF. \b0\par\par\uc2 198 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 September 2000\par 03:02\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2000 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, Sept 27 (Reuters) - Senior Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  (MOF) official Zembei Mizoguchi said on Wednesday the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market was taking the Group of Seven's (G7) rare joint \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  last week to defend the euro seriously. The beleaguered euro rose about one percent against both the dollar and the yen overnight on concern that the G7 would again buy euros as they did on Friday in the first joint \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in five years. \par \par \uc2  "We regard the market as taking last Friday's joint \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  seriously," Mizoguchi, head of the ministry's International Bureau, told reporters. The European \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has stabilised since the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  after repeatedly setting record lows against both the dollar and the euro. G7 finance ministers and central bankers in a communique on Saturday expressed shared concern over the euro's weakness and vowed to cooperate as necessary. \par \par \uc2  "The G7's stance is just as it is written in the communique," Mizoguchi said. "There is absolutely no change." \par \par \uc2  He also played down a looming Danish referendum on whether to join the euro bloc, saying it was a domestic issue rather than one concerning the credibility of the euro. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010821dw9r06h50\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
