\par\par \b \uc2  Veteran Greenspan aide sees Fed hike within 6 months. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Daniel Sternoff \par \uc2 704 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 25 September 2000\par 21:42\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2000 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  NEW YORK, Sept 25 (Reuters) - A longtime top advisor to Federal Reserve Chairman Alan Greenspan said on Monday he saw a good chance the Fed will hike interest rates again by early 2001 to quash inflation risks. \par \par \uc2  Michael Prell, who retired in July after 13 years as the Fed Board research director and 30 years at the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , said high oil costs, tight labour markets and the potential for a sharp fall in the dollar were all prime inflation threats even as the economy cools. \par \par \uc2  "I think there is a considerable chance we will see some rise in the funds rate in next six months as the Fed responds to evidence of further deterioration in inflation trends," Prell told a teleconference sponsored by ISI Group. \par \par \uc2  As the chief domestic economic and financial forecaster for the policy-setting Federal Open Market Committee (FOMC), Prell played a key role in shaping the Fed's economic assessments during his tenure. \par \par \uc2  He said the Fed was unlikely to raise the key 6.5 percent federal funds rate for a seventh time since June 1999 at its Oct. 3 meeting - the last gathering before the Nov. 7 presidential elections. But the Fed will keep markets on notice that inflation remains the paramount risk to the economy. \par \par \uc2  Soaring oil costs, which on Monday fell below 10-year peaks after the United States decided to tap its strategic reserves to avert a fuel crisis, could yet push core inflation and wages higher, Prell said. \par \par \uc2  The Fed's latest "Beige Book" survey of economic conditions suggested that "employers' hesitancy to engage in price competition for workers may be breaking down," Prell said. \par \par \uc2  And even though sharp gains in U.S. labour productivity - a key factor restraining wage inflation - were likely to be sustained at a pace of at least 3 percent a year, Prell said "scarce workers are beginning to exploit their leverage by demanding more money, not merely assurances of job security". \par \par \uc2  FED WORRIES ABOUT SHARP DOLLAR DROP \par \par \uc2  The strong dollar, which has helped control inflation by keeping import costs low and forcing exporters to slim down to compete, could pose an inflation risk should it retreat from current decade highs in trade-weighted terms. \par \par \uc2  "The FOMC has been worried for some time that that U.S. current account deficit is on an unsustainable path," Prell said. \par \par \uc2  "The dollar could at some point begin to drop rather sharply. Certainly that would be unwelcome at a time when resource utilization is already high and posing an inflation risk," he added. \par \par \uc2  Given growing Fed concerns that the dollar's strength could turn, Prell said it was somewhat "anomalous" that the United States last week joined the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  and \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to prop up the ailing euro. \par \par \uc2  He said the Fed may have felt it had some wiggle room because the dollar had strengthened beyond its expectations. \par \par \uc2  WALL STREET TOO OPTIMISTIC \par \par \uc2  Prell said he had to "mildly question" stock and bond valuations based on market optimism that the Fed is done tightening credit, or may even need to lower rates to keep the economy from falling into recession. \par \par \uc2  "A steady or declining funds rate would be far more likely if the stock market were to decline noticeably, something investors obviously aren't anticipating at this juncture," Prell said. \par \par \uc2  He said he agreed with estimates that the economy had probably slowed from a blistering 5.3 percent growth rate in the second quarter to an annual pace closer to 2.5 to 3 percent growth in gross domestic product (GDP) currently. \par \par \uc2  While he said he saw room for a renewed pickup in growth moving into 2001, Prell does not foresee GDP increases moving back up into the 5 percent plus range in the near term. \par \par \uc2  "My bottom line is that there is an inflation risk in the outlook," Prell said. \par \par \uc2  "While moderation in GDP growth and the recognition on the part of FOMC members that anti-inflation policy has tended to be overdone in past cycles will give them some pause, I suspect that the FOMC members will feel the need to act," he said. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010821dw9p06s5g\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
