\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Euro supported as  \highlight22\uc2 intervention \highlight\uc2  jitters persist. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Sabrina Ghani \par \uc2 725 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 25 September 2000\par 12:23\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2000 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, Sept 25 (Reuters) - The euro was steady against the dollar on Monday amid lingering fears that the Group of Seven nations will repeat last week's concerted \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to prop up the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . While traders said they remained unconvinced that the euro had found a floor, they were reluctant to aggressively challenge the G7 after the policy makers said, in a communique released following Saturday's meeting, that they would continue to cooperate in exchange markets as appropriate. \par \par \uc2  "I think there is some residual degree of scepticism about the effectiveness of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but price action today indicates that the market is wary of taking on the G7," said Neil MacKinnon, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Merrill Lynch in London. \par \par \uc2  "If we see further slippage in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , I am pretty sure the G7 will show up again and shorting euro is not a sensible strategy." \par \par \uc2  The euro was hovering just above the day's lows around $0.8765 amid an absence of any further \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  action on Monday. It was more than two cents below Friday's post-\b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  peaks, but still up about four percent from last week's record lows around $0.8440. \par \par \uc2  Against the yen, the euro was trading around 94.50, about two yen below Friday's post-\b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  highs. \par \par \uc2  Financial markets were stunned by the concerted \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to buy euros on Friday, particularly with the unexpected participation of the United States - which even after the action maintained that its "strong dollar" policy was unchanged. \par \par \uc2  It was the first such large-scale coordinated action since 1995, and traders had little doubt central banks would not easily let it be seen as a failure. \par \par \uc2  MARKET SEEN TESTING EURO DOWNSIDE \par \par \uc2  On Monday, fears of further G7 \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  were kept alive by comments from senior G7 officials. However, traders said it was a matter of time before the market began to test the euro's downside to see if they will back up their comments with action. \par \par \uc2  The Italian Treasury Minister Vincenzo Visco said the G7 was now expecting financial markets to pick up the baton on the euro after Friday's concerted \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile, the Japanese Vice Finance Minister Toshiro Muto said \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  would continue to watch euro movements carefully and would take action if needed. \par \par \uc2  "So far it has been quiet but there is a good chance that we will test lower again because people want to see if the central banks will show up again," said a traders at a German bank in Frankfurt. \par \par \uc2  Traders added that it was too early to say that the euro was out of the woods as the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's pullback from the post-\b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  peaks on Friday was seen as a proof of the underlying pressure on the euro, whose losses against the dollar since its launch in 1999 reached 28 percent last week. \par \par \uc2  Investment in U.S. businesses and securities continued to attract European capital, while concerns lingered over the euro-zone inflation rate, which was running above the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's target. \par \par \uc2  Furthermore, dealers scented a whiff of differences within the G7 as the U.S. commitment to a strong dollar was seemingly at odds with a euro-supportive policy. \par \par \uc2  Treasury Secretary Larry Summers reaffirmed his commitment to a "strong dollar" policy after Friday's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "I think investors have to want to buy the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and to buy it just because you think the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is around the corner is never enough to provide a durable recovery," said Steve Barrow, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Bear Stearns in London. \par \par \uc2  Elsewhere, the Danish crown tested multi-week peaks against the euro ahead of Thursday's referendum on Denmark's euro membership. \par \par \uc2  Danish euro opponents led supporters 45-41 in latest IFO poll, 50-42 in Megafon poll, 46-42 in Gallup poll. \par \par \uc2  Traders said they will continue to closely scrutinise all Danish polls issue ahead of a referendum. They added that an increase in "No" votes could provide further blow to investors' confidence in the euro. \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile, the dollar was holding within a half yen of Friday's one-month highs around 108.15 yen. It was little changed after rating agency Standard & Poor's said it saw no reason to cut Japan's triple-A rating. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010821dw9p06pap\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
