\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  Asia: G7 Euro-Supportive Steps May Lift Dlr/Yen Too \b0\par\par\uc2  By Ron Harui \par \uc2 825 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 25 September 2000\par 07:30\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2000, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    A Dow Jones Newswires Column\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par TOKYO -(Dow Jones)- The Group of Seven's efforts to bolster the euro -\par expected to continue over the next few weeks - are likely to have a\par byproduct: a dollar rise versus the yen.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  The dollar could rise to Y109 by the end of the week, en route to a test of Y110 further out. A break of that level would put the dollar's high for the year of Y111.73, set in February, back on the radar screen, analysts say. \par \par \uc2  The factors lined up in the dollar's favor range from economic fundamentals - the U.S. economy is far more robust than Japan's - to tacit approval of such a move from official quarters; the U.S. needs a strong dollar to ensure financial market stability, while Japan would welcome a weak \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support exports and cap deflationary pressure in the economy. \par \par \uc2  Even mechanics are on the dollar's side: for the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  on the euro's behalf, it must first sell yen for dollars before then selling the purchased dollars for euros. \par \par \uc2  And few doubt that last Friday's dramatic attempt to bolster the euro will be the last. \par \par \uc2  "It is obvious that G7 will \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  again (to support the euro)," said Kenneth Landon, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Deutsche Bank in Tokyo. "Monetary authorities are particularly sensitive about their 'credibility' with market participants, so they aren't likely to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  once and go away." \par \par \uc2  Credibility is the key. Friday's coordinated \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which brought together the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the U.S. Federal Reserve, the BOJ, the Bank of England and the Bank of Canada - was the first coordinated action by the major central banks since 1995. The rarity of such action underscores concerns that if monetary authorities fail in an effort to stabilize a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the risk of a rout increases exponentially. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    Euro Retreats From High\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Although Friday's move pushed the euro higher across the board, the\par common \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  later lost a lot of its gains. Around 0400 GMT in Asia\par Monday, the euro was quoted at $0.8812, up from $0.8784 in New York late\par Friday but well below Friday's intraday high of $0.9040.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par In a communique published after their meeting in Prague Saturday, G7\par monetary officials said the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reflected "shared\par concern...about the potential implications of recent movements in the\par euro for the world economy."\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The weakening euro was a source of problems around the world. U.S.\par financial market players fretted over the implications for corporate\par earnings if U.S. exports to Europe suffered. In Japan, there was\par widespread concern about the massive losses financial institutions were\par absorbing on investments in Europe. Elsewhere, there was a growing sense\par that a slowdown in the U.S. due to the dollar's sharp rise against the\par European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  would damp U.S. demand for imports from Asia and Latin\par America, undermining economies in those regions.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Now that the G7 has committed itself to defending the euro, G7 central\par banks will have to stay the course, analysts say.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "If the G7 disappoints the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets by avoiding further\par euro-supportive \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  this week, we could easily see another\par sell-off of the euro against other \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  such as the yen," said\par Shuji Takano, assistant \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  manager at ABN AMRO Bank in\par Tokyo.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Ryohei Muramatsu, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and treasury manager at Commerzbank\par in Tokyo, said, "The ECB has finally used its last weapon in its arsenal\par to support the euro. It will now have to keep doing so as market forces\par will undoubtedly test its resolve."\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par For this week, analysts expects the euro to move between Y92.50 and\par Y96.50, with the market's focus on whether the G7 takes further\par euro-supportive steps in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "The issue is whether the G7 nations take a defensive or aggressive\par stance towards \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . If it is the former, the euro could resume\par its fall in the near future. If it is the latter, the euro will probably\par stabilize," a Japanese city bank dealer said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Provided the G7 sticks to its guns and the ECB backs its position up\par with euro-supportive monetary policy, the euro will likely recover from\par here, analysts say. For this week, analysts expect the euro to move\par between Y92.50 and Y96.50 and between $0.8550 and $0.9050.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par As for the dollar-yen, Landon of Deutsche said the U.S. \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  could\par rise from current levels around Y107.70 by one and a half yen by the end\par of the week.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "I think the dollar could rise to as high as Y109.20 by the end of\par this week and to above Y110 within the next few months," Landon said.\par -By Ron Harui, Dow Jones Newswires; 813-5255-2929;\par ron.harui@dowjones.com\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  4258 5095 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010805dw9p04mmh\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
