\par\par \b \uc2  Tale Of 2  \highlight22\uc2 Currencies \highlight\uc2 :Can Strong Euro,Strong Dlr Coexist? \b0\par\par\uc2  Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par \uc2 1,137 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 25 September 2000\par 03:30\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2000, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  By Damian Milverton \par \par \uc2  PRAGUE -(Dow Jones)- U.S. Treasury Secretary Lawrence Summers has now stamped his \b \highlight22\uc2 interventionist\b0 \highlight\uc2  credentials with his decision to join the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the Bank of Japan Friday in bolstering a sickly euro. But the overriding question for traders is: does the successor to the hard-nosed, market-savvy Robert Rubin have what it takes to turn a single bold move into the salvation of the European \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 ? \par \par \uc2  For the answer, officials at the meetings of the group of seven industrialized nations strove to invoke memories of 1998, when the U.S. - with Summers as Rubin's deputy - acted to resuscitate the yen with such success that 18 months later the BoJ was \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  to halt a yen resurgence that might have stifled the nascent Japanese economic recovery. \par \par \uc2  The U.S., some at the Prague meetings argued, is so adverse to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  they won't allow it to fail when they eventually succumbs to the necessity to meddle with the market as a favor to old allies. \par \par \uc2  Rather than question whether concerted \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  will arise once again, the more important factor for any \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  manager is likely to be just why the joint \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  occurred at all, given the traditional U.S. reluctance on \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the logical questioning of just how Washington can maintain a strong dollar policy when it has acted to support another major \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  at the dollar's expense. \par \par \uc2  Rather than asking why the U.S. risks undermining its strong dollar policy - a better question for those in the markets is what prompted the U.S. to bid up the euro? \par \par \uc2  Unfortunately for strategists, the key trigger for the ECB and its well-resourced allies in the U.S. and Japan is most likely to be found in the amount of speculative action in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market. \par \par \uc2  According to the similarly-worded statements out of Europe, \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the U.S. Friday after the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , they acted out of concern for the impact of a weakening euro on the global economy. \par \par \uc2  If a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is merely reflecting the economic fundamentals of its home economy, \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is seldom regarded as a panacea. According to the U.S. model for \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy, solid economic policies - clearly laid out and forcefully implemented - should underpin \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  stability and strength. Look at the U.S. economy and trace its growth against the path of the U.S. dollar. \par \par \uc2  Speculative "Bubble" May Have Been Key Factor \par \par \uc2  When a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  outpaces economic fundamentals - either positive or negative - then the answer to its behavior must lay elsewhere, or so goes the U.S. thinking on \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . When the euro's slide went from the glumly consistent to sharply negative in August and September, the answer to what was the catalyst went from the realm of economics to that of speculation. \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  speculation, to be specific. \par \par \uc2  C. Fred Bergsten, a former Treasury official and now the director of the Institute of International Economics in Washington D.C., said in an interview last week before the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  that 10%-20% of the euro's decline since January 1999 was "pure bubble." \par \par \uc2  Support for such a theory abounded in the daily \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reports from major international banks. \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  analysts went from advocating long positions in the euro to a new-found pessimism within a matter of days as it breached preset technical levels below $0.9000. \par \par \uc2  A hankering for long-euro positions among \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  traders dissolved and was replaced by a misgiving that perhaps the euro was doomed to a medium-term dose of anemia. Accordingly, short positions began to look considerably more attractive. \par \par \uc2  However, given the importance of monetary union in the drive to forge a unified Europe, officials in Brussels, Tokyo and Washington were favorably disposed to undertaking whatever action was deemed necessary to sparing Europe fresh political and economic turmoil - especially given the backdrop of a slowing U.S. economy and a slow and unsteady recovery in \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  remains a key weapon in the fight against \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , purely because of the psychological trauma it can cause speculators, a quality never lost on the U.S. despite its apparent aversion to tinkering with market forces. \par \par \uc2  Stanley Fischer, first deputy managing director of the IMF and himself a self-confessed anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 interventionist\b0 \highlight\uc2 , said Sunday that Friday's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support the euro was already "clearly an initial success" because of the uncertainty it will generate among traders as to the chances of a repeat performance. \par \par \uc2  Summers himself sent a clear message that he can easily overcome his instinctive aversion to \b \highlight22\uc2 interventionist\b0 \highlight\uc2  methods by declaring that there is "no contradiction" between his advocacy of a strong dollar only hours after causing the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken as much as four cents versus the euro. \par \par \uc2  Developing countries - already fearful of contagion effects from a weaker euro in their own \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  and consumer prices - were none too pleased that Summers had apparently undone much of the positive effects of Friday's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by reaffirming his continuing desire to see a strong dollar. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  Aim A Stronger Euro, Not Weaker Dollar \par \par \uc2  U.S. officials publicly shrugged. The strong dollar policy, they argue, has worked in luring productive investment to the U.S. while insulating the economy from imported inflation. Supporting another major \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  from speculative attack or an otherwise unjustified slump shouldn't alter the strong dollar policy because the object of the exercise wasn't to weaken the dollar but to boost a counterpart. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  traders have argued that this is a disingenuous argument, akin to the Treasury having its cake and eating it too by rescuing the yen without causing an accompanying slide in the dollar. \par \par \uc2  No one in Washington monetary circles is prepared to argue that \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in favor of the euro will lead to the kind of spectacular rebound experienced by the yen in 1998. Europe's political diversity - within a common \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  system - poses all kinds of challenges that Berlin, Paris and Brussels are yet to resolve with respect to the common \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Additionally, the pace of economic and structural reform also is spotty across the continent, further complicating Europe's attempt to present a clear message on its economic direction. \par \par \uc2  Achieving such aims will take time and it poses significant threats to European economic evolution and the well-being of its trading partners more widely - none more so than a U.S. burdened by a record current-account deficit. \par \par \uc2  Summers and Federal Reserve Chairman Alan Greenspan are universally acknowledged as risk averse and are therefore not expected to relax with a single venture back into the realm of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , should events convince them otherwise. \par \par \uc2  -By Damian Milverton, +41-79-474-1304; damian.milverton@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010805dw9p04mbm\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
