\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Euro regains ground amid G7 wariness. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Lisa Jucca \par \uc2 767 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 September 2000\par 10:07\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2000 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  (Updates throughout, adds quotes and latest prices, changes dateline and byline) \par \par \uc2  LONDON, Sept 22 (Reuters) - The euro held within sight of one-week highs against the dollar and the yen on Friday, as investors were wary of being short of euros ahead of a Group of Seven meeting this weekend. \par \par \uc2  Its gains against the dollar were also said to be assisted by sterling's powerful rise against the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  against the backdrop of persistent merger and acquisition flows and talk of British rate rises. \par \par \uc2  The euro was seen gaining against the yen after a three percent fall in Tokyo's leading Nikkei index on Friday dented the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's sentiment. \par \par \uc2  The European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's monthly report, released on Thursday, was also seen as mildly supportive for the battered single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  as it vented irritation over the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's continuous weakness. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said a euro-supportive statement was seen as the most likely outcome from this weekend's G7 meeting in Prague. But most observers doubted the powers would lift the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  unless they resorted to co-ordinated \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "We had a very sharp euro rally overnight on short covering and on a certain hesitation ahead of the G7 meeting," said Stephen Hannah, head of research at IBJ International in London. \par \par \uc2  "From a speculative point of view the market has recognised that a lot of the negative has been priced in." \par \par \uc2  By 0740 GMT the euro was bobbing around $0.86 according to Reuters data, having climbed more than 1.5 percent from record low around $0.8460 hit on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  The euro peeped above 92 yen on Friday, setting one-week highs and putting some distance between it and its record low around 90 according to Reuters data. \par \par \uc2  Data coming out of German states showed an increase in consumer prices, but analysts were doubtful on whether this could have a beneficial effect on the euro because of the potential damage a further rate hike would do to European stock markets. \par \par \uc2  END OF EURO'S WOES NOT YET IN SIDE \par \par \uc2  Analysts were doubtful that the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  could hold its gains if the G7 failed to take decisive action. \par \par \uc2  Doubts about a the U.S. Federal Reserve taking part in any G7 rescue plan were reinforced overnight when U.S. Treasury Deputy Secretary Stuart Eizenstat repeated that the administration's strong dollar policy remained unchanged. \par \par \uc2  "This was not the fundamental turnaround people were looking for," said Ryan Shea, economist at Bank One in London. \par \par \uc2  "Some people have been a little bit cautious ahead of G7 and took out any short position they had. But if the G7 does not result in anything along the lines of co-ordinated \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , selling should continue next week." \par \par \uc2  Analysts said one option was that the ECB would anticipate a bearish reaction to the G7 by \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  with the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Monday morning or after Danish euro referendum on September 28 in case of a "No" vote. \par \par \uc2  In both cases the central idea was that the ECB would try to catch the market when it was overly short of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "They've got to inflict pain on the bears; and that's been their problem," argued a bank dealer in Tokyo. \par \par \uc2  "They need the speculative market to be very, very short to cause pain. But up to now there's been few speculators to punish as the selling is real money flows, M&A's and the like. And how can you squeeze a company that wants to buy in the U.S.?" \par \par \uc2  For the time being, a fall in the U.S. hi-tech Nasdaq index was giving some respite to the euro as it was hitting the dollar. \par \par \uc2  The dollar edged up to 106.90 yen from one-week lows around 106.25 hit on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  The market was keeping an eye on the oil market after U.S. Energy Secretary Bill Richardson said on Thursday that President Bill Clinton was needing a decision on releasing oil from the nation' strategic stocks to cut back soaring crude prices. \par \par \uc2  Crude oil, which is priced in dollars, eased back from 10-year highs hit earlier in the week in anticipation of increase in output and analysts said it could have an effect on the foreign exchanges if the fall was to continue. \par \par \uc2  The dollar was also suffering against sterling. Investors bought initially sterling against euros in anticipation of sterling-supportive flows and such gains lifted sterling to 1-1/2-week peaks above $1.44. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010821dw9m06yu8\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
