\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Euro hits one-week highs vs dollar, yen, eyes G7. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Carolyn Cohn \par \uc2 847 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 September 2000\par 12:32\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2000 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, Sept 22 (Reuters) - The euro rose to one-week highs against the dollar and the yen on Friday as investors positioned themselves for euro-supportive moves from a Group of Seven meeting of finance ministers this weekend. \par \par \uc2  A lively two-day rally in the pound against the dollar, helped by talk of sterling-positive merger and acquisition flows and a near-term British rate rise, also fed into euro gains, traders said. \par \par \uc2  "The euro is up because of caution and short-covering going into the G7 meeting," said Geraldine Concagh, economist at AIB Group Treasury in Dublin. \par \par \uc2  "But we are unlikely to get anything more than words of comfort from the G7, so there is a very strong risk we could be retreating again next week." \par \par \uc2  The euro was trading a little below its one-week highs, at $0.8617/22 EUR=) and 92.29/34 yen EURJPY=R at 0935 GMT, according to Reuters data, nearly two cents and more than two yen above record lows hit on Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  An overnight profit warning by U.S. chip maker Intel and a three percent fall in Tokyo's leading Nikkei index .N225) on Friday threw the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  an additional lifeline, traders said. \par \par \uc2  Nasdaq 100 futures NDZ0) traded on Globex were quoted down 92 points at 0935 GMT, implying a poor open to the Nasdaq index in New York. \par \par \uc2  FINE WORDS SEEN FROM G7 \par \par \uc2  Analysts said a euro-supportive statement was seen as the most likely outcome from this weekend's G7 meeting in Prague. \par \par \uc2  German Chancellor Gerhard Schroeder said on Friday that he and Italian Prime Minister Guiliano Amato were interested in a strong euro and that the European Union economy was in good condition, but his words failed to provide any added boost for the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  More supportive for the euro was the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's monthly report, released late on Thursday, in which the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said the weakness of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  posed a risk to price stability. \par \par \uc2  Inflation data released Thursday and Friday by the German states of Saxony, Hesse and Bavaria also raised the likelihood of a euro-zone rate hike, analysts said. \par \par \uc2  Consumer prices in Saxony rose by 2.2 percent year-on-year, while the year-on-year gains in Hesse and Bavaria were 2.4 percent and 2.5 percent respectively. \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile, the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said on Friday the continued strong growth of imports, primarly fuelled by rising oil prices and the depreciation of the euro, continued to be the driving force behing the euro-zone's narrowing trade surplus, to 35.5 billion euros, and widening current account deficit, to 14.0 billion euros, in the January-July period. \par \par \uc2  But most observers doubted there would be any long-lasting lift to the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  unless G7 resorted to co-ordinated \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Doubts about the U.S. Federal Reserve taking part in any G7 rescue plan were reinforced overnight when U.S. Treasury Deputy Secretary Stuart Eizenstat repeated that the administration's strong dollar policy remained unchanged. \par \par \uc2  "This was not the fundamental turnaround people were looking for," said Ryan Shea, economist at Bank One in London. \par \par \uc2  "Some people have been a little bit cautious ahead of G7 and took out any short position they had. But if the G7 does not result in anything along the lines of co-ordinated \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , selling should continue next week." \par \par \uc2  Analysts said one option was that the ECB would anticipate a bearish reaction to the G7 by \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  with the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Monday morning or after the Danish euro referendum on September 28 in case of a "No" vote. \par \par \uc2  The latest influential Green's poll on Friday showed Danish euro opponents leading supporters by 51-44 percent. \par \par \uc2  Sterling rose to two-week highs against the dollar above $1.45 GBP=) and four-month highs against the euro beyond 59.50 pence EURGBP=) amid M&A-related flows this week centring on talk German power giant RWE AG was close to buying Britain's Thames Water Plc in a 4.1 billion pound deal. \par \par \uc2  Sterling was also benefiting from this week's encouraging monthly trends survey from the Confederation of British Industry, minutes showing a tight 5:4 vote to keep rates unchanged at the Bank of England's September monetary meeting, and the ruling euro-friendly Labour party's slide in opinion polls, analysts said. \par \par \uc2  The dollar, meanwhile, benefited from the euro's gains against the yen, rising above 107 yen JPY=) to its highest in two days. \par \par \uc2  The market was keeping an eye on oil prices after U.S. Energy Secretary Bill Richardson said on Thursday that President Bill Clinton was nearing a decision on releasing oil from strategic stocks. \par \par \uc2  Crude oil, which is priced in dollars, eased back from 10-year highs hit earlier in the week in anticipation of an increase in output and analysts said it could have an effect on foreign exchanges if the fall was to continue. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010821dw9m06y6f\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
