\par\par \b \uc2  Euro clings to gains, dares not hope for G7 aid. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Wayne Cole \par \uc2 1,079 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 September 2000\par 08:18\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2000 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, Sept 22 (Reuters) - The euro clung to its short-covering gains on Friday as caution ruled ahead of a Group of Seven meeting, even though most observers doubted the powers would or could float a liferaft for the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The dollar and yen were discomforted by steep falls in U.S. and Japanese equities following a profit warning from tech bellweather Intel, while a stunning overnight rally by sterling gave all the European \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  a fillip. \par \par \uc2  But while analysts paid respect to the G7, they could not help but feel that Monday would bring naught but anti-climax. \par \par \uc2  "The most we expect is a few words of encouragement for the euro," said Ken Landon, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Deutsche Bank in Tokyo. "Politically it's just too difficult for the U.S. to do any more this close to an election." \par \par \uc2  "And if that's so, we should see the euro resume its retreat next week," he added. \par \par \uc2  The euro was trying to maintain as much altitude at possible at $0.8601 having climbed all the way from $0.8460 on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  The euro even extended its gains on the yen to 91.95 putting some distance between it and the week's record low around 90.05. \par \par \uc2  Doubts about a G7 rescue plan were reinforced overnight when U.S. Treasury Deputy Secretary Stuart Eizenstat repeated that the administration's strong dollar policy remained unchanged. \par \par \uc2  But that did not stop the rumour mill working. \par \par \uc2  "It's been very quiet today and idle hands make loose lips," said a U.S. bank dealer. \par \par \uc2  Among the many rumours cited was talk that the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would anticipate a bearish reaction to the G7 by \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  with the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Monday morning. Or that they would wait for the Danish euro referendum on September 28 and \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  on a "No" vote. \par \par \uc2  In both cases the central idea was that the ECB would try and catch the market when it was overly short of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "They've got to inflict pain on the bears; and that's been their problem," argued the dealer. \par \par \uc2  "They need the speculative market to be very, very short to cause pain. But up to now there's been few speculators to punish as the selling is real money flows, M&A's and the like. And how can you squeeze a company that wants to buy in the U.S.?" \par \par \uc2  Then again the euro's long fall was finally having repercussions in the United States. \par \par \uc2  For one, Intel cited weakness in its European \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  when making its profit warning, a warning which sent the Nasdaq futures contract limit down on Globex with a fall of 2.4 percent. \par \par \uc2  Dealers noted European equities would not be spared, but argued that the United States had the most to lose since its assets had to stay attractive to entice the offshore money needed to fund the country's swelling trade deficit. \par \par \uc2  Likewise, a three percent tumble in Tokyo stocks on Friday was viewed as yen-negative since it boded ill for the country's tenuous recovery and even helped lift the dollar on the yen. \par \par \uc2  The dollar edged up to 106.90 yen from 106.54 in New York on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  The euro's bounce overnight owed much to a stunning rally by sterling, which was easily the biggest gainer overnight of all the major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Ironically it was lifted first by heavy corporate buying of sterling for euro - dealers estimated it to be worth around four billion euros - which was believed to be M&A related. \par \par \uc2  But while this lifted the pound on the euro, it resulted in much larger gains on the dollar and yen because the market was much more short on these crosses having sold sterling aggressively in recent weeks. \par \par \uc2  Thus the pound leapt almost two percent on the dollar to stand at $1.4376 and 2.2 percent on the yen to 153.73. \par \par \uc2  Dealers also reported confused talk about either a U.K. or U.S. hedge fund being caught by a margin call on a loss-making euro/yen position. \par \par \uc2  The odd part of the rumour was that this fund was supposed to have funded its position by borrowing in sterling and that it had to cover this by buying the pound back in size. But dealers were non-plussed given borrowing costs in sterling were much higher than in, say, the Swiss franc or yen. \par \par \uc2  "The talk is all over the market but the details just don't make sense," complained one dealer. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  bid prices at 0551 GMT. All data taken from Reuters with percent change calculated from the daily U.S. close at 2130 GMT. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \tab \tab \tab   Last     US Close  % Chg  YTD %   1999 Cls\par \tab \tab \tab \tab      21 Sep.\par \tab \tab \tab   -----------------------------------------\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par   Euro/dlr   0.8599   0.8614   -0.17   -14.61   1.0070\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par   Dlr/yen    106.90   106.54   +0.34    +4.59   102.21\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par   Euro/yen   91.92    91.40   +0.57   -10.84   103.10\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par   Dlr/swiss  1.7619   1.7610   +0.05   +10.80   1.5902\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par   Stg/dlr    1.4374   1.4378   -0.03   -11.14   1.6176\par 