\par\par \b \uc2  Euro/dlr options vols surge, nervous market eyes G7. \b0\par\par\uc2  By David Bailey \par \uc2 621 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 September 2000\par 21:59\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2000 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  CHICAGO, Sept 22 (Reuters) - Implied volatility in one-month euro/dollar options surged to a record high near 18.00 percent on Friday after coordinated \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to prop the sagging euro. \par \par \uc2  The euro bounce above 90.00 cents quickly cooled, but one-month volatility held most of its bid into the afternoon, driven by uncertainty over the meeting of finance ministers and central bankers from the Group of Seven nations this weekend. \par \par \uc2  Traders noted good demand for short-dated options with the market on edge over whether the G7 will take additional steps to support the euro in coming sessions. \par \par \uc2  The reaction of back-end options was just as significant, traders said. Implied volatility in one-year options actually softened to about 13.85 percent on Friday afternoon from 14.0 percent on Thursday with an easing of uncertainty. \par \par \uc2  "The U.S. is willing to partake (of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ) even if it isn't signalling a change in policy," one \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  options trader said. \par \par \uc2  Treasury Secretary Lawrence Summers's statement that the United States has not changed its strong dollar policy suggests that U.S. participation in the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  aimed to limit the potential for a crisis rather than weaken the dollar, he added. \par \par \uc2  The euro bounce justified the recent steep bid in one-month options above 15 percent, but the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's resilience depends on whether the market perceives the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to be as credible a threat to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  as the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 , said Alan Revercomb, options strategist at IDEAglobal. \par \par \uc2  "The options had been pricing the potential for some kind of discontinuous move, which is exactly what has come," he said. "It repeats the same pattern that we have seen since the (euro) inception where big volatility spikes come on bounces." \par \par \uc2  The one-month euro/dollar volatility opened near 15.3 percent, jumped to a mid of about 17.75 percent after the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and was quoted near 17 percent Friday afternoon. \par \par \uc2  The one-month 25-delta risk reversal favoured euro calls at 0.25/0.5 in the afternoon, a reversal from the sharp premium of 0.7/1.0 afforded euro puts before the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The options market had been willing to pay up to 1.5 more for euro puts than calls as the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  fell to lifetime low against the dollar near 84.40 cents this week. However, it was the calls that proved more valuable. \par \par \uc2  Revercomb said it would be hard to justify one-year volatility much higher than it was on Friday given the euro's real performance. \par \par \uc2  "You take (Friday) out of the data sample and you are not getting actual volatility that justifies intermediate to back-end volatilities any higher than this," Revercomb said. \par \par \uc2  Central bankers need to show a real commitment to the euro through a strong G7 statement or more \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , or else the euro could slide back to lifetime lows, traders said. \par \par \uc2  "Vols have been bid up for (the threat of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ), so if this move is seen as the major reaction of this bounce, then vols will be seen as far too high," Revercomb said. "Markets tend to have a very short memory." \par \par \uc2  One-month vols will come under pressure if the euro holds a "safe area" from about 88.00 to 90.00 cents, but a retracement toward the lifetime lows would offer potential for substantial volatility over several days, Revercomb said. \par \par \uc2  Cable's move to safer levels above $1.40 may take some pressure off front-end volatility. Sterling bids can drop much more quickly than in euro/dollar, Revercomb said. Potential for instability below $1.40 produced much of the bid. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010821dw9m06s23\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
