\par\par \b \uc2  G7 stitched together euro deal at high speed. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Ashley Seager \par \uc2 638 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 September 2000\par 19:22\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2000 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  PRAGUE, Sept 22 (Reuters) - It took a frantic round of phone calls around the globe in the early hours of Friday for the Group of Seven rich countries to put together their concerted \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support the flagging euro. \par \par \uc2  Sources close to the action told Reuters the move, designed to try and halt the seemingly unstoppable slide in the fledgling \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's value against the mighty dollar, was strongly supported by all concerned. \par \par \uc2  G7 countries - Britain, Canada, France, Germany, Italy, Japan and the United States - bought euros and sold dollars in the first such \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in five years shortly after midday European time on Friday. \par \par \uc2  Japanese Finance Minister Kiichi Miyazawa said he had approved \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's role in the joint \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  from the airport as he left to go to Prague for a G7 gathering on Saturday. \par \par \uc2  When asked why the move was made before the meeting itself, he said, "It was natural that we did it as soon as possible, even a day earlier." \par \par \uc2  One G7 \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  official, who travelled to Prague on Friday morning for the G7 meeting, was caught out by the move. \par \par \uc2  "I didn't know it was going to happen, it must have been decided this morning. I was surprised in the end that the U.S. had come on board because it had been looking negative for the past few days," he said. \par \par \uc2  NO ONE SAYING HOW MUCH THEY SPENT \par \par \uc2  While most G7 sources were remaining tight-lipped about exactly how the move was stitched together and in particular about how much cash they were throwing at the euro, Italian Treasury Minister Vincenzo Visco said European finance ministers had asked the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to set things in motion. \par \par \uc2  Financial markets had been full of talk of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  with the euro hitting new lows against the dollar on an almost daily basis and since European finance ministers and \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governors held a meeting in Versailles earlier this month and discussed the euro. \par \par \uc2  It became clear on Friday they decided something had to be done, and quickly. \par \par \uc2  "The \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  of the ECB puts into action the decisions taken by EU finance ministers in Versailles, and subsequently supported by the (bank) governors, to strengthen the euro in the interests of Europe," said Visco. \par \par \uc2  The British government made clear, should anyone have doubted it, why the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was done. \par \par \uc2  "This is a coordinated move by the G7 authorities. As a member of the G7, we share the concern about the potential implications of recent movements in the euro exchange rate for the world economy," said a Treasury spokesman. \par \par \uc2  "We were asked, and agreed, to participate. We believe that \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is only effective if public and concerted as this was," he added, but declined to comment further. \par \par \uc2  Few pundits had expected \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  this soon because they thought the U.S. government would be reluctant to get involved in such action with only weeks to go to a presidential election. \par \par \uc2  And that remained a mystery on Friday. \par \par \uc2  U.S. Treasury Secretary Lawrence Summers declined to comment on the reasons for U.S. involvement but said the government's policy of favouring a strong dollar had not changed in spite of its involvement in selling dollars for euros. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  follows an announcement by the ECB last week that it would use interest earned on its reserves to buy euros. \par \par \uc2  That briefly put a floor under the troubled euro but it soon crashed to new lows of below $0.8450. Whether the latest bout of buying will reverse the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's slide remains to be seen. \par \par \uc2  It was only around a cent higher at $0.8800 in late European trading, down from a high just after the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  of a little over $0.9000 cents. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010821dw9m06kvk\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
