\par\par \b \uc2  ANALYSIS-G7 needs to back money with words to win euro battle. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Swaha Pattanaik \par \uc2 702 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 September 2000\par 18:22\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2000 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, Sept 22 (Reuters) - The Group of Seven leading industrial nations on Friday called a halt to the euro's protracted slump and will this weekend have to show they intend to win the fight they have started with the foreign exchanges. \par \par \uc2  The European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  spearheaded a euro-buying spree which saw the United States, Europe, and \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  coordinate \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  for the first time in five years, just a day before a G7 meeting in Prague. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealers who had bet the United States would not get embroiled in such action so close to the U.S. presidential elections were aghast and their immediate scramble to buy euros sent the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  soaring. \par \par \uc2  But a battle of wills is underway, with traders running for cover each time a new wave of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  breaks but re-emerging to sell euros after the central banks disappear. \par \par \uc2  The G7 nations will this weekend have to show they are prepared to keep battering \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealers if they want to keep up their winning streak of the past 15 years when it comes to wars over exchange rates. \par \par \uc2  "This is the shocker and the aftershock is still to come," said David Bloom, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at HSBC Markets in London. "We are going to have to wait for a statement from the G7 - these are the things the market will pick up on later and the sustainability of the move depends on it." \par \par \uc2  CENTRAL BANKS USUALLY WIN \par \par \uc2  The ECB said on Friday other members of the G7 had supported its euro-rescue \b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2  because of a shared concern about the potential implications of its fall for the world economy. \par \par \uc2  The euro has fallen nearly 30 percent against the dollar since its January 1999 launch and shed about a third of its value against the yen in that time, a slide which the ECB said on Thursday was increasingly out of line with economic reality. \par \par \uc2  The United States, Japan, Germany, France, Italy, Britain, and Canada, are now expected to jointly reiterate concern about Europe's single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  after their weekend meeting. \par \par \uc2  If the wording of their past communiques is anything to go by, they will also pledge to monitor exchange rates and continue to cooperate as needed - the clearest signal that they will keep \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the euro's defence if need be. While the ECB may be a young bank, the G7 have a wealth of experience of fighting the foreign exchanges and know just what is expected of them. \par \par \uc2  Their success in lifting the dollar from post-World-War-Two lows against the yen and record lows against the German mark in 1995 flew in the face of the doom mongers who had been as down on the dollar then as they are on the euro today. \par \par \uc2  Repeated rounds of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  turned the foreign exchanges around on their head, just as they did after the 1985 Plaza Accord which saw industrial nations join forces to push the dollar down or the 1987 Louvre Accord which did the reverse. \par \par \uc2  "Central banks always win in the end in my experience and the shock impact of this \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was good," said Eddie Tofpik, head of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  at ADM Investor Services in London. \par \par \uc2  "But it is early days, and this has just put the ante up." \par \par \uc2  Dealers may be in the mood for a fight, but if anything, the international authorities have an even better chance of winning than they did in the 1990s, given the large hedge funds who used to take them on are no longer the force they used to be. \par \par \uc2  In 1992, speculative attacks by such large speculative players as George Soros, forced sterling out of the European exchange-rate mechanism but such funds are all but absent from the foreign exchanges these days. \par \par \uc2  "\b \highlight22\uc2 Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  always works in the long run and central banks have generally got bigger and deeper pockets than the markets these days," said Nick Parsons, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Commerzbank in London. \par \par \uc2  "It was different in 1992 when hedge funds took on the central banks but this time central banks are kicking into an open goal." \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010821dw9m06kh9\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
