\par\par \b \uc2  Euro Grain-Euro rally confuses EU export strategy. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Greg Frost \par \uc2 631 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 September 2000\par 16:35\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2000 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  PARIS, Sept 22 (Reuters) - When European, U.S. and Japanese central banks \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Friday to prop up the sickly euro, they may have complicated the European Union's attempts to sell wheat and barley without export subsidies, analysts said. \par \par \uc2  The euro jumped more than five percent after the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the U.S. Treasury and the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  bought the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  on world \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  may have relieved euro-zone consumers, who have seen their buying power slashed by the European single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's recent descent, but it has also reduced prospects for the European Commission to keep a lid on its budget. \par \par \uc2  The ailing euro made EU grain more competitive on the world market, which allowed Brussels to export large chunks of grain without relying on export subsidies, as it had previously. \par \par \uc2  The less money the Commission spends in export subsidies, the more money it saves for its central farm budget, which is around 40 billion euros a year. \par \par \uc2  Since the start of the 2000/01 crop marketing year on July 1, the Commission has awarded licences to export almost half a million tonnes of free-market soft wheat with no export refunds. \par \par \uc2  During that time, the EU has also sold more than 1.2 million tonnes of publicly held barley and almost 800,000 tonnes of publicly held wheat at prices that are defined by world trade rules as unsubsidised. \par \par \uc2  A Commission source declined to estimate how much money the EU has saved this crop year by selling without subsidies, but grain industry analysts said the amount was substantial. \par \par \uc2  EURO'S WEAKNESS KEY \par \par \uc2  "The biggest reason the EU has been able to export (with no subsidies) is the weak euro," said Gerald Mason, analyst with the London-based Home Grown Cereals Authority. \par \par \uc2  Mason noted that this time last year, the EU was having to pay an export refund of 34 euros per tonne to bring the price of EU soft wheat in line with that of competing U.S. soft red winter wheat at the Gulf of Mexico. \par \par \uc2  Lately, however, Brussels has been granting wheat for export with a refund of zero euros. \par \par \uc2  "A lot of the reason for the zero-euro refund was the fact that in the space of a year, the euro has lost 20 percent of its value against the dollar," Mason said, noting that U.S. wheat at the Gulf was valued this week at around $98 per tonne fob - exactly where it was a year ago. \par \par \uc2  While the frail euro was the biggest reason allowing the EU to export without refunds, analyst said the recent reforms in EU agriculture policy had also helped. \par \par \uc2  Prices for European Union \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  grain dropped 7.5 percent on July 1 - the first step in a series of farm reforms adopted in 1999 to save the EU money and make EU grain more competitive on the world market. \par \par \uc2  STRONGER EURO COULD CHANGE EU WHEAT STANCE \par \par \uc2  Grain analysts said that if the euro strengthened against the dollar, the Commission might have to resume giving refunds on wheat exports. \par \par \uc2  "If the dollar stays weaker against the euro, the Commission may have to look as early as next week at whether it wants to resume refunds on wheat exports," Mason said. \par \par \uc2  Noel Fryer, an analyst at website agrinews-online.com, agreed that if the euro strengthens in coming weeks, the EU will probably not be able to continue selling wheat without subsidies. \par \par \uc2  "There is no doubt that the EU's ability to export without subsidies was primarily due to the weakness in the euro," Fryer said, noting that each tick upwards in the euro added around a dollar to the value of EU wheat. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010821dw9m06j2t\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
