\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\uc2  tactics gives clues to ECB, G7 methods. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Carolyn Cohn \par \uc2 861 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 September 2000\par 19:15\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2000 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, Sept 22 (Reuters) - \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealers got the first taste on Friday of the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's methods of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  on the foreign exchanges and now know what might come their way in the weeks ahead. \par \par \uc2  The ECB, which joined forces with other Group of Seven industrialised nations to buy euros, dealt directly with a range of banks at a time when the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was already rising. \par \par \uc2  It did so in a public \b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2  which was immediately flagged by an official statement. This was the first time since 1995 that the U.S, \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  and Europe had \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  jointly. \par \par \uc2  Central banks started buying the euro shortly after 1100 GMT, when the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was already three cents above record lows it had set against the dollar on Wednesday and rising. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is typically more successful if the market is already moving in the right direction and when it has the element of surprise on its side. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said this was the case on Friday as the market was already covering short euro positions amid fears of euro-supportive action after the weekend meeting of the G7 finance ministers. \par \par \uc2  In the event, they were overtaken by the central banks' shock attack before the meeting. \par \par \uc2  "Some large sell orders in dollar/Swiss triggered gains in the euro, and then the ECB came in," said a dealer from a UK bank in London. \par \par \uc2  The euro was trading under $0.8740 when dealers began to report that central banks were piling into the market. \par \par \uc2  European dealers said central banks had dealt from around $0.8740 to around $0.8850 in the first wave of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which was followed by at least one or two more. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the relevant countries \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in their own \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  cross rates - the United States in euro/dollar, the Japanese in euro/yen, the Canadians in euro/Canadian dollar and the British in euro/sterling. \par \par \uc2  The first round of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  saw the euro surge to two-and-a-half week highs above $0.90. A high of $0.9040 was seen on electronic broking systems, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  "Whether or not you deal with central banks, you can tell when there is \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  because the two-way market dries up. EBS becomes one-sided and all the other prices have gone," said a trader from a European bank in Paris. \par \par \uc2  SEVERAL CENTRAL BANKS INVOLVED \par \par \uc2  In addition to the ECB, the Fed and the BOJ, confirmation of coordinated action came from the Bundesbank, the Bank of France, the British Treasury and the Bank of Canada. \par \par \uc2  The Swiss National Bank said it did not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 , putting the responsibility for the action firmly in G7's court - the G7 powers are the United States, France, Germany, Italy, Japan, Britain and Canada. \par \par \uc2  "There were so many central banks involved - the ECB, the Fed, the BOJ, the Bundesbank, the Bank of France - but who knows who else?" said Paul Mackel, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  analyst at Dresdner Kleinwort Benson in Frankfurt. \par \par \uc2  "There might be other ones just sitting on the sidelines, maybe some of the smaller European central banks." \par \par \uc2  European bank dealers said the Bank of Italy also \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2 . They said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was spotted buying the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  for yen but added it was not clear whether these purchases were on its own behalf or for Japan. \par \par \uc2  Euro/yen jumped as far as 96.60 yen , according to Reuters data. \par \par \uc2  But investors used higher euro levels as an excuse to sell, and dealers said the central banks came back into the market shortly after 1200 GMT when euro/dollar was back around $0.8840. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the size of the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Friday was hard to gauge, though one said it was "medium to large-sized", compared with past \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Another suggested the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  volumes on Friday could have amounted to at least $3.0 billion, but declined to be more specific about which central banks had spent the most money. \par \par \uc2  At last count, the net foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves of the European system of central banks was 260.9 billion euros, giving them ample scope to prop up the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The size of Japan's euro purchases were not immediately clear but could amount to a sizeable chunk of the total if its past forays into the foreign exchanges are any guide. \par \par \uc2  The Finance Ministry has said in recent months that Japan sold 1.385 trillion yen ($12.88 billion) yen for dollars in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in one day alone, on April 3, 2000 when it was combatting yen strength. \par \par \uc2  The Bank of Japan acts as the Finance Ministry's agent in the foreign exchanges. \par \par \uc2  The euro fell to $0.88 by 1612 GMT, and traders said G7 powers would need to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  again to stop the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's rot setting in once more. \par \par \uc2  "Welcome to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  - it's a two-way trade," said a trader from a U.S. bank in London. \par \par \uc2  "A lot of exporters are taking their cue to sell the euro from here, but it is going to go higher again - they will have to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  again." \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010821dw9m06fn4\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw200\pich25\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3000\pichgoal375
