\par\par \b \uc2  Treasurys Close Lower in Wake of  \highlight22\uc2 Central-Bank Intervention \highlight\uc2  in  \highlight22\uc2 Currency \highlight\uc2  Markets \b0\par\par\uc2 791 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 September 2000\par 15:18\par \uc2  Dow Jones Business News \par \uc2 DJON\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2000, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  A WSJ.com News Roundup \par \par \uc2  NEW YORK -- Treasurys finished modestly lower Friday, as dollar-denominated securities came under pressure after the central banks of the U.S., \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 , England, Canada and the euro zone \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets to support the euro. \par \par \uc2  In addition, an anticipated meltdown in the stock market, spurred by a revenue warning from Intel Corp., didn't materialize. \par \par \uc2  Late Friday, the 10-year Treasury note was down 5/32, or $1.5625 per $1,000 face value, at 99 7/32. Its yield, which moves inversely to its price, rose to 5.850% from 5.829% late Thursday. \par \par \uc2  At the longer end of the curve, the 30-year Treasury bond slipped 7/32 to 104 20/32, yielding 5.912%. The two-year note dropped 4/32 to 100 3/32, yielding 6.070%. \par \par \uc2  Treasurys were hurt as the dollar lost ground against the euro. A weaker dollar makes dollar-denominated U.S. securities less attractive to foreign investors. \par \par \uc2  The dollar sank immediately after the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said the Federal Reserve, the Bank of England, the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  and other central banks cooperated in Friday's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to buy euros, the first ever carried out by the ECB. The central banks took joint action after the euro fell to a series of lows against the dollar and the Japanese yen earlier this week. However, after soaring above 90 U.S. cents immediately after the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the euro's initial gains against the dollar were more than cut in half. \par \par \uc2  Traders noted that there likely would be selling in two-year Treasury notes by central banks involved in the joint \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Mark Mahoney, market strategist of UBS Warburg in Stamford, Conn., said he hadn't seen any selling of notes related to the euro, but he added such sales may carry through the first couple of days of next week. \par \par \uc2  Early Friday, short-term Treasurys moved higher, with the yield on the two-year note dipping below 6% for the first time in nine months, as some investors abandoned stocks for the relative safety of government-backed securities. Asian stock markets sank as technology shares tumbled after Intel's downward revision to its third-quarter revenue forecast. \par \par \uc2  Intel said late Thursday that it expects its third-quarter revenue to fall below previous expectations primarily because of weaker demand in Europe. Intel said it expects to report third-quarter revenue 3% to 5% higher than second-quarter revenue of $8.3 billion. Analysts had expected revenue to grow between 8% to 12% in the quarter. \par \par \uc2  Traders had positioned themselves for a flight-to-quality bid in hopes stock investors would seek a safe haven in the relative stability of Treasurys. But when stock investors took the Intel warning somewhat better than had been expected, the bid in the Treasury market eroded. \par \par \uc2  "What we are seeing is a disappointment in the flight-to-quality trades" related to equities, said Jim Caron, market strategist with Merrill Lynch & Co. in New York. "This morning's bullishness ... had more to do with a [potential] disastrous opening in stocks," and when that didn't happen, Treasurys began losing support, he said. After losing more than 3% in intraday trading, the tech-heavy Nasdaq Composite Index finished down just 25 points, or 0.7%, while the Dow Jones Industrial Average gained 82 points. \par \par \uc2  How Treasurys might trade going forward remains a bit uncertain. A number of market participants said the stock-market's influence could loom larger. But at the same time, they added that investors' yield-curve positioning, which has driven significant levels of volatility over the last week, will likely continue. \par \par \uc2  "Clearly, the market is reacting to the inversion [positioning] and everyone scrambling to figure out what it means.... It's a period of significant uncertainty and it's going to take some work" to figure which direction is best going forward, said Jack Malvey, market strategist with Lehman Government Securities in New York. \par \par \uc2  Mr. Malvey said he is expecting some choppy trading over the coming weeks. But he added that some of the market's most unfriendly factors, such as decade-high oil prices, may well be cresting. That would be a positive for longer-dated issues, which have been wracked by fears of inflation created by the high oil prices. But oil prices have plunged for two straight sessions, losing more than $2.50 a barrel. Friday's losses came as President Clinton ordered a limited release of oil from the nation's emergency reserve in an effort to relieve the tight supply situation. \par \par \uc2  Copyright (c) 2000 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Dokument djon000020020330dw9m01hmj\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
