\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Forex \highlight\uc2  Options: Euro Volatilities Ease After  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 442 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 September 2000\par 20:59\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2000, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  NEW YORK -(Dow Jones)- Volatilities implied by euro/dollar one-month \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  options eased slightly in late New York trade compared with five hours earlier as the euro gradually slipped against the dollar following concerted \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support the euro. \par \par \uc2  The euro retreated from its \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 -induced highs early Friday in New York, following a leap of over four cents on the coordinated \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the U.S. Federal Reserve Board, the Bank of England, the Bank of Canada and several European banks. \par \par \uc2  Although dealers remained cautious about prospects for \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  during the U.S. session to restore the euro to over 89 cents, no further \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  activity was seen. \par \par \uc2  At 2:30 p.m. EDT (1830 GMT), the euro was at $0.8793, lower compared with $0.8869 earlier in London but up from $0.8483 late in New York Thursday. It was at Y95.03, down compared with Y95.33 earlier in London but up from Y90.42 in New York late Thursday. \par \par \uc2  The dollar was at Y108.02, higher compared with Y107.38 earlier in London and Y106.60 in New York late Thursday. \par \par \uc2  In the over-the-counter market, one-month at-the-money dollar/yen options imply volatility of 9.80%-10.10%, up from 9.40%-9.80% earlier in London and unchanged from 9.80%-10.10% in New York late Thursday. \par \par \uc2  One-month euro/dollar option volatilities are at 16.40%-16.80%, down slightly compared with 16.50%-17.00 earlier in London and up from 15.20%-15.50% in New York late Thursday. \par \par \uc2  One-month risk-reversals favor yen call options against the dollar at 1.80%-2.00% compared with 1.70%-2.00% earlier in London and 1.90%-2.20% in New York late Thursday. Risk-reversals measure the market preference for either put or call options by the premium or discount applied. \par \par \uc2  Euro/dollar risk reversals are neutral at -0.10% to +0.30% compared with -0.15% to +0.15% earlier in London. The figures indicate that buyers pay a premium for both euro puts and euro calls, although euro calls are most costly. Late Thursday in New York, the risk reverals favored euro puts at 0.80%-1.00%. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Sterling puts are favored over dollars by 0.10%-0.40% compared with\par 0.0%-0.30% earlier in London and 0.80%-1.00% in New York late Thursday.\par -By Christine Richard, Dow Jones Newswires; 201-938-4384\par  christine.richard@dowjones.com\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  1077 4127 44427 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010805dw9m04or1\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
