\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Euro bears hold fire ahead of G7 meeting. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Deepthi Wickremasinghe \par \uc2 744 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 September 2000\par 12:33\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2000 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, Sept 21 (Reuters) - The euro backed off the previous day's record lows against the dollar and the yen on Thursday as it sought support from the Group of Seven finance ministers' meeting this weekend to save it from new lows. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said ongoing negative sentiment towards the euro and the economies of the euro zone was balanced only by wariness about what the G7 finance ministers might come up with when they meet in Prague on Saturday. \par \par \uc2  "G7 ministers have a chance of trying to do something for the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  - there has been plenty of verbal \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and markets are tired of this talk," said Fabio Fraschetti, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Banca Nazionale del Lavoro in London. \par \par \uc2  The euro was at 90.61/71 by 0941 GMT according to Reuters data, having retreated from a brief foray above 91 yen. It was up more than half a yen from record lows set on Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  Against the dollar, the euro had eased from a peak of $0.8550 according to Reuters data, which had taken it more than one cent away from Wednesday's record troughs. \par \par \uc2  While traders deemed it prudent to square positions ahead of the G7 meeting in Prague on Saturday, there was scepticism that the finance ministers would come up with a convincing rescue package for the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "We do expect them to say something about the euro but nothing dramatic, but even so it is normal for short term accounts to close their positions so we are getting a squeeze higher," said Jesper Dannesboe, chief \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Dresdner Kleinwort Benson in London. \par \par \uc2  "The bias for now should be to wait for rallies and then sell the rallies on a view that nothing major will come out of the G7." \par \par \uc2  Analysts said the euro's gains were also likely to be limited after Wednesday's news that the Ifo Institute's west German business climate index fell for the third month in a row, and of slower-than-expected gross domestic product growth in Italy. \par \par \uc2  UNREALISTIC FOR ECB TO ACT ALONE \par \par \uc2  Japanese financial daily Nihon Keizai Shimbun reported on Thursday that the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  (ECB) may have started considering solo \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to stem the euro's slump. \par \par \uc2  The report quoted ECB sources as saying it was unrealistic to persist with the option of tripartite \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  with the United States, \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  and Europe. \par \par \uc2  But the report also said it was unclear whether the ECB would conduct unilateral \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to buy euros as there were concerns within the ECB that such action would have only a limited effect. \par \par \uc2  The market shrugged off the report as the sourcing was vague. \par \par \uc2  "One of the main drivers of euro weakness is still portfolio flows," said Dannesboe at Dresdner. \par \par \uc2  "If they \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  now the signal they are sending to the market is that they aim to slow the decline. They can't really trigger a trend reversal because there is no speculative bubble to burst." \par \par \uc2  U.S. Treasury Secretary Lawrence Summers underlined Washington's reluctance to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  late on Wednesday by reiterating support for the U.S. "strong dollar" policy. \par \par \uc2  But Summers also said he was "closely watching" world \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and energy markets, leaving the possibility of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  open, analysts said. \par \par \uc2  News of slower-than-expected economic activity in Japan in July briefly propped up the dollar against the yen in early trade. But the dollar soon retreated to near 106.50 yen, where large stop-loss sales are said to be lurking. \par \par \uc2  Economic activity in Japan slowed 1.0 percent in July from the previous month, the Ministry of International Trade and Industry's (MITI) all-industries index showed on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  A core component of the all-industries index, the tertiary sector, mainly comprising service industries, fell 1.1 percent. \par \par \uc2  These indices are seen as useful pointers to gross domestic product data, suggesting that the third quarter got off to a soft start, though analysts remain reasonably confident of a bounce back in August and September. \par \par \uc2  The dollar was last at 105.44/49, just above just above one-week lows set on Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  The New Zealand dollar dropped to new record lows of $0.4010, according to Reuters data, in tandem with weakness in the euro and the Australian dollar. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said there was a short-term risk of the sickly kiwi falling below US$0.40. REUTERS. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010821dw9l06t5u\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw200\pich25\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3000\pichgoal375
