42BC84C70C9887B236AF1643CDC717CBD3B2C814349CB139F3B86C3172CB21F0A67544176DF4D14827AD34751100003B}}
\par\par \b \uc2  Euro Hits New Low Against Dollar as Case for  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\uc2  Mounts \b0\par\par\uc2 688 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 19 September 2000\par 13:47\par \uc2  Dow Jones Business News \par \uc2 DJON\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2000, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  A WSJ.com News Roundup \par \par \uc2  NEW YORK -- The embattled euro dropped to another low against the dollar Tuesday as pressure mounted for some level of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  from the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Late Tuesday, the euro bought 85.14 U.S. cents, down from 85.42 cents late Monday in New York. In afternoon New York trading, the euro fell to an all-time low of 84.74 U.S. cents. \par \par \uc2  The dollar traded at 106.93 yen, little changed from 106.97 yen late Monday in New York. The British pound fetched $1.4087, up from $1.4055 late Monday. \par \par \uc2  The euro's previous low of 84.80 cents was hit during European trading. It also hit another all-time low of 90.60 yen. But the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  bounced back amid reports of buying by euro-zone central banks, presumably as part of the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's widely publicized plan to rebalance its foreign-\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves. The bank announced those purchases of euros last week. \par \par \uc2  As the trading day concluded in Europe and European banks exited the market, the euro slumped to another low during the New York trading day. Since its debut in January 1999, the euro has lost more than 27% of its value against the dollar and 31% of its value against the yen. \par \par \uc2  Comments from Deutsche Bundesbank President Ernst Welteke failed to help the euro. Mr. Welteke said he expects the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to recover after the U.S. presidential elections in November. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's slide has attracted attention from international bodies. International Monetary Fund economic counselor Michael Mussa set out the case for market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , saying the euro's weakness poses a risk to all \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  regions, including the U.S. and Japan. \par \par \uc2  He said that with economic and financial fundamentals only accounting for about half of the euro's slide against the dollar since the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's launch, government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support the euro would be valid. \par \par \uc2  "If not now, when?" he said in reference to whether present circumstances warrant \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Conditions that favor \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  are rare, but they do arise, he added. \par \par \uc2  Mr. Welteke wouldn't comment on possible \b \highlight22\uc2 central-bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  action, saying speculation about \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  on \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets before the event would render such an \b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2  ineffective. \par \par \uc2  Ministers from the Group of Seven industrial nations are expected to refer to the euro's weakness during their weekend meeting. Japan's Finance Minister Kiichi Miyazawa said he wants to find out what other G-7 finance ministers think of the euro's drop and appeared to hint that his country would be willing to support \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  of behalf of the euro, saying "\b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  will cooperate for the sake of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  stability" in a "flexible" way. \par \par \uc2  With concern growing over the euro, some analysts now see U.S. participation in any \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  as more likely. \par \par \uc2  "Before the euro was seen as a European problem or even a Japanese problem because of the weak euro/yen [and its effect on Japan's exports], but now a much lower euro from here is a significant event for world financial markets and it's very unsettling," said Patrick Brodie, vice president of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  at Sumitomo Bank in New York. "Treasury is going to have to really look closely at this thing, and I think it's a matter of time before we have \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ." \par \par \uc2  Mr. Brodie said he believes a break below 80 cents would trigger concerted \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and such a break looks increasingly likely in the coming weeks with the Danish referendum on adopting the euro set for Sept. 28. \par \par \uc2  But other analysts are skeptical of the longer-term impact of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  given steady flows out Europe and into the dollar. \par \par \uc2  "It is one thing to support a weak \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , it's another to try to cap a strong \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," said Marc Chandler, chief \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Mellon Financial Corp., noting that the dollar's strength has been responsible for much of the euro's decline. \par \par \uc2  Copyright (c) 2000 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Dokument djon000020020330dw9j01gn6\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
