\par\par \b \uc2  As US Feels Euro Pain, Some See  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\uc2  More Likely \b0\par\par\uc2  By Robert Flint and Christine Richard \par \uc2 1,144 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 19 September 2000\par 23:06\par \uc2  Capital Markets Report \par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2000, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  NEW YORK -(Dow Jones)- Conventional wisdom has it that the Clinton Adminstration would be crazy to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets before the November presidential elections. \par \par \uc2  But now that the euro's relentless decline has rattled some marquee names in the U.S. stock market, a few market watchers are urging people to abandon conventional wisdom. These contrarians believe that joint U.S.-European \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken the dollar could be forthcoming sooner than most in the market think. \par \par \uc2  What's changed is that the strong dollar, once considered a key source of support for U.S. asset markets and thus something no Treasury Secretary would want to weaken, is now seen in some quarters as a burden. Earnings downgrades from Gillette Co. (G), Colgate-Palmolive Co. (CL), McDonalds Corp. (MCD) and Dupont Co. (DD) this month were all partly blamed on the weak euro. \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  problems are also being cited as a reason for broader stock market weakness, although many equity analysts see the euro as a secondary issue to bigger concerns such as the decade-high price of oil. \par \par \uc2  Most analysts still say that \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  before the Nov. 7 vote would create the kind of volatility that Vice President Al Gore would not want to be associated with during his presidential campaign. But some are betting that the newfound awareness of U.S. exposure to euro risks alters the political dynamics. These people say the euro has already become a threat to global financial stability and, for that reason, the Treasury may soon authorize the Federal Reserve to jointly come to the euro's rescue with  other Group of Seven central banks. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par            Weak Euro Now A Global Problem\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "Before the euro was seen as a European problem or even a Japanese\par problem because of the weak euro/yen (and its effect on Japan's exports)\par but now a much lower euro from here ($0.8500) is a significant event for\par world financial markets and it's very unsettling," said Patrick Brodie,\par vice president of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  at Sumitomo Bank in New York.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "Treasury is going to have to really look closely at this thing and I\par think it's a matter of time before we have \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ."\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Brodie says he believes a break below 80 cents would trigger concerted\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and that such a break is increasingly likely in coming\par weeks, with the Danish referendum on adopting the euro set for September\par 28. A Gallup Poll published Tuesday showed 49% of those surveyed would\par vote against joining the euro, while 37% favored the euro and 14% were\par undecided. It was the second straight day that Danish euro-skeptics\par topped euro-supporters by a comfortable margin.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par With concerns mounting over the prospects of "No" vote in Denmark\par sending negative signals on the longer term prospects for the euro, the\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  slipped below 85 cents for the first time Tuesday, reaching a\par new all-time low of $0.8476 in the New York afternoon. It recovered\par slightly after that, but at $0.8514 late in the day, it was still more\par than 27% below its launch level on Jan. 1, 1999.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Jawboning by European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and euro-zone financial officials\par hasn't had a noticeable positive effect on the euro in recent days and\par stronger measures will be needed to alter the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's course, traders\par said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par That's why many have turned their focus to a weekend meeting of G7\par monetary officials, which could result in a statement of concern about\par the euro and set the stage for further action, such as joint\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , analysts say.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par On Monday, German \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  president Ernst Welteke acknowledged\par euro weakness would be on the agenda at the Prague meeting. He and other\par euro-zone officials have stated \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is a tool which could be\par used at any time.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par But U.S. support for a G7 statement of concern is one thing, a\par commitment to concerted \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is another. So far, Treasury\par Secretary Lawrence Summers has not wavered from his oft-repeated mantra\par that a strong dollar is in U.S. interests and it remains widely\par acknowledged that this administration sees \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  as a flawed\par tool.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par That's not to say extreme situations in global markets can prompt a\par change in policy - after all, Summer's predecessor Robert Rubin backed\par joint \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  with \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in 1995 to boost the dollar against the\par yen. But no one's certain that the euro's decline represents an extreme\par case and there is known to be considerable resistance to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par within  Treasury.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    Fed Participation Could Be Seen As Partisan Move\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par One reason for that resistance is that \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is often seen as\par as unworkable when the market is positioned against it. That could set\par monetary authorities up for a failure that could damage their\par credibility over the longer term.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Now is not the right time to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , says Marc Chandler, chief\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategist at Mellon Financial Corp., because capital flows\par into the strong U.S. dollar are dead set against the euro.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "They would want to catch the euro when it is being squeezed higher,"\par said Chandler. "It is one thing to support a weak \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , it's another\par to try to cap a strong \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ."\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Both Treasury and the Fed are also cognizant of the considerable risks\par to which they would expose the U.S. economy if the dollar were to\par weaken. In addition to keeping inflation in check by reducing the cost\par of imports, the strong dollar has until now kept much needed investment\par flowing into the U.S.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "An \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  could inject risk that foreign investors currently\par don't perceive," Chandler said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Bill Rhodes, Chief Investment Strategist at Williams Capital Group LP,\par said \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  would be little more than a Band-Aid, since the euro's\par problems are fundamental in nature, a result of both its structure and\par the policies chosen by the nations involved.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "For the Fed to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  ... as a kind of international act of\par charity, would literally be throwing good money after bad," Rhodes said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The biggest danger, from the Fed's point of view, is that any move it\par makes could be interpreted as partisan, said Philip Jefferson, an\par economics professor at Swarthmore College and formerly a research\par economist at the Fed.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "This is the type of attention the Fed does not want," Jefferson said.\par "The Fed does not want to fight this battle, especially under the\par glaring lights of a presidential political campaign. Better to wait it\par out on the sidelines."\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par But what about relief for the beleaguered single \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ?\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "The euro will have to fend for itself, for now," he said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    By Robert Flint and Christine Richard, Dow Jones Newswires;\par 201-938-4408\par    robert.flint@dowjones.com\par    christine.richard@dowjones.com\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  1074 4164 \par \par \uc2 Dokument cm00000020010804dw9j00nnl\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
