\par\par \b \uc2  Japan Wants Weak Euro To Be A Core Topic At G7 -Sources \b0\par\par\uc2 382 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 September 2000\par 06:15\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2000, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  TOKYO -(Dow Jones)- Japanese Finance Ministry officials want to make the recent weakness of the euro against the dollar and yen one of the core topic at next week's meeting of Group of Seven finance ministers and \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governors in Prague, G7 sources say. \par \par \uc2  The sources, who asked not to be named, told Dow Jones Newswires that it hasn't yet been decided whether the G7 officials will express concern about the euro's sharp slide in a communique expected to be issued after the meeting, which is tentatively scheduled for Sept. 23. \par \par \uc2  However, "I believe all of the G7 nations share the view that the recent weakness of the euro is not desirable for the global economy. Japan wants to discuss the issue as one of main topics at the G7 meeting," a G7 source said. \par \par \uc2  The Prague G7 meeting will be closely watched by \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market players amid speculation that G7 officials might announce their readiness to support the single European \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Indeed, after hitting all-time lows against the dollar and the euro this week, the euro has managed to trim some of its losses due to fears that Euro-zone officials might \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support the European \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  worries have been fueled by remarks by top European officials such as European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  president Wim Duisenberg, Deutsche Bundesbank President Ernst Welteke and European Union Commission President Romano Prodi, who have all expressed concern about the weakness of the euro. \par \par \uc2  However, Duisenberg and Welteke have said nothing about when and in what kind of situation \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  might occur. And \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  traders say that even if euro-supportive \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  were conducted, it would only have limited impact if the action isn't conducted jointly with the U.S. and Japan. While many traders think that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  is ready to conduct joint \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  with the ECB, U.S. authorities are believed to be reluctant to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to sell dollars so soon before presidential elections in November. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Delegates at G7 meetings routinely discuss the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  rates of\par member nations. However, it's unusual for a single \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to become a\par main topic.\par    -By Hidenobu Kageyama, Dow Jones New; 813-5255-2937;\par hidenobu.kageyama@cor.dowjones.com\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010805dw9e04gpp\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
