\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Japan \highlight\uc2  Mizoguchi: No Comment On Joint Euro  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 169 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 September 2000\par 02:29\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2000, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  TOKYO -(Dow Jones)- The head of the international bureau at the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  said Wednesday that the euro wasn't reflecting the euro-zone's economic fundamentals but couldn't comment on the possibility of joint \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "Europe is saying that the euro weakness isn't reflecting economic fundamentals and isn't good for Europe. We share this view," Zembei Mizoguchi told reporters. \par \par \uc2  Asked about the possibility of concerted \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  action, he said he had "no comment." He added that Japan was in regular contact with European financial authorities. \par \par \uc2  European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  President Wim Duisenberg, in his regular testimony before the European Parliament on Tuesday, reiterated the euro's level doesn't reflect euro-zone fundamentals. \par \par \uc2  While saying that he couldn't comment on current dollar-yen levels, Mizoguchi reiterated that an excessively strong yen against both the dollar and euro was undesirable. \par \par \uc2  -By James Simms, Dow Jones Newswires; 813-5255-2929; james.simms@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010805dw9d04hq3\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
