\par\par \b \uc2  Japan unlikely to attempt solo euro rescue-dealers. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Masayuki Kitano \par \uc2 687 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 September 2000\par 07:35\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2000 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, Sept 12 (Reuters) - The euro's ever-more dismal performance has Japan worried, but not yet worried enough for its authorities to launch a solo rescue attempt, dealers and analysts said on Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  Tokyo is painfully aware of the damage being inflicted on Japanese investors by the euro's falls, but fears \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's behalf would be futile unless it is backed by European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  action, they said. \par \par \uc2  "Japanese authorities are unlikely to conduct solitary euro-buying, yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," said Takayuki Togawa, manager for Tokai Bank Ltd's international treasury division. \par \par \uc2  "This is because such \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , no matter how much they try, will not be effective unless the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  actually decides to take action." \par \par \uc2  The euro stood at 91.09/17 yen in early Tuesday afternoon trade, after plumbing a record low of 90.83 yen overnight as well as falling to fresh lows against the dollar and Swiss franc. \par \par \uc2  Dealers have no doubt that Japanese authorities want to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to stem the euro's falls against the yen, which are hurting Japanese investors with huge exposure to euro-zone assets as well as Japanese exporters. \par \par \uc2  "I don't think anyone in Japan is happy about the euro's falls against the yen," said a dealer for a European bank. \par \par \uc2  But it is a difficult situation for \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  because solitary euro-buying \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  would likely be futile, while dollar/yen has been stable and doesn't seem to merit \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , he said. \par \par \uc2  While the euro has fallen four percent against the yen since the start of last week, the dollar has risen to 106.13/21 yen from 105.65 yen in early Tokyo on September 4. \par \par \uc2  Dealers say, however, that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  may \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to buy dollars if it were to fall towards the 104.09 yen level. That level was cited by Japanese manufacturers in the Bank of Japan's "tankan" business sentiment survey as the exchange rate used to draw up their business plans for the current fiscal year. \par \par \uc2  The BOJ, acting as an agent of the Finance Ministry, last \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets on April 3, when it \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to buy dollars starting from levels near 103 yen. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  last \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to buy euros against yen on March 8 from levels around 101.70 yen. \par \par \uc2  SCEPTICISM ON \b \highlight22\uc2 INTERVENTION\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2  The euro set a fresh lifetime low of $0.8566 on Monday, due in part to the lack of direct reference to \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in a statement released by euro-zone finance ministers after a meeting on Friday. \par \par \uc2  The finance ministers and the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  reiterated their "common concern" that the current level of the euro does not reflect economic fundamentals. \par \par \uc2  The statement resembled one issued after a euro group meeting in May, which was not followed by any ECB action. The ECB has not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets since the euro's launch. \par \par \uc2  Mixed signals from European officials reinforced the market's scepticism. \par \par \uc2  "The euro group meeting only underscored the lack of a concerted European position towards the euro's falls," said Taisuke Tanaka, for Credit Suisse First Boston in Tokyo. \par \par \uc2  But some traders speculate that European authorities could step in at any time in view of the euro's relentless fall. Indeed, some said there were rumours overnight that European monetary authorities were selling U.S. Treasury holdings in preparation for euro-buying \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  DOLLAR MORE IMPORTANT \par \par \uc2  Dealers say Japanese authorities seem more preoccupied with moves in the dollar against the yen. \par \par \uc2  Motoshi Imura, senior manager for the Bank of Tokyo-Mitsubishi Ltd said falls in the euro had less impact on Japan's economy than falls in the dollar because of the smaller size of net exports to the euro zone compared to the United States. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  data shows that Japan's trade surplus with western Europe totalled 1.88 trillion yen ($17.7 billion) in January to June, about half the size of its 3.68 trillion yen surplus to the United States ($34.7 billion) over that period. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010821dw9c08emj\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
