\par\par \b \uc2  WRAPUP 1-Battered euro deaf to chorus of support. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Ian Geoghegan \par \uc2 905 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 September 2000\par 19:30\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2000 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  (Wraps separate stories) \par \par \uc2  BRUSSELS, Sept 12 (Reuters) - The euro remained stubbornly downbeat Tuesday despite senior EU monetary chiefs attempts to talk up Europe's battered single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and stave off a growing crisis of confidence. \par \par \uc2  European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  President Wim Duisenberg led the charge to bolster the euro by insisting the euro's exchange rate defied economic fundamentals. And he admitted that rising oil prices and the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's malaise were "cause for concern". \par \par \uc2  Ernst Welteke, President of Germany's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , weighed in, too, saying the euro was undervalued and its appreciation was in the common interest. \par \par \uc2  "Monetary policy as well as the entire euro-zone economic policy have a fundamental interest in a strong external value for the euro and believe that the current level is fundamentally unjustified," the Bundesbank chief said in a speech. \par \par \uc2  The euro has been forced to lifetime lows by speculators drawing an unflattering comparison between the 11-nation common \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  zone and the United States, which is growing faster, is seen as more dynamic, and is sucking in European and other overseas investment. \par \par \uc2  During Duisenberg's quarterly testimony to the European Parliament's Economic and Monetary Committee, the euro spiked up to test 87 cents, but by 1630 GMT had backtracked to around 86 cents, not far above the earlier all-time low of $0.8553. \par \par \uc2  NO CLUES ON \b \highlight22\uc2 INTERVENTION\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2  Financial markets are on red alert for any ECB action to prop up the ailing euro, which has lost almost a third of its value against the dollar since its launch last year. \par \par \uc2  The depreciation has lifted exports, but has fuelled inflation and is threatening the region with higher interest rates which could blunt its recovery. \par \par \uc2  Worry over the euro's performance on \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets has also played against a Danish government campaign to join monetary union, due to be put to the vote in a referendum on September 28. \par \par \uc2  One opinion poll gave the "no" camp a clear five-point lead on Tuesday. A second put the "yes" vote two points ahead. \par \par \uc2  Welteke said \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was a tool at the ECB's disposal but, like Duisenberg, he stressed it would not be discussed prior to its deployment. \par \par \uc2  Austria's Finance Minister Karl-Heinz Grasser, however, said euro zone finance ministers should have been more explicit last week about their willingness to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support the euro at the appropriate time. \par \par \uc2  European Commission President Romano Prodi also entered the fray, telling Germany's Stern magazine that it was in the interests of the United States to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  with other central banks to support the euro. \par \par \uc2  A Commission spokesman confirmed that Prodi, asked whether \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the U.S., \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  and Europe could help the euro, replied: "Something like this is never simple, especially not before a United States election, even if we are now at a point where it would be in America's interests. Nobody can live forever with such a current account deficit". \par \par \uc2  The spokesman was quick to add that Prodi was not "offering advice to America's policymakers." \par \par \uc2  INFLATION MAY HAVE PEAKED \par \par \uc2  Declining to respond to one call for him to resign, Duisenberg said pressure from oil prices and the weak euro was likely to keep euro zone inflation running above the desired two percent into early 2001, but he believed it may have peaked at around a recent 2.4 percent. \par \par \uc2  "From a monetary point of view, (recent exchange rate developments) are clearly a cause for concern as they add to upwards pressure on the consumer prices in the euro area...(but) they do not appear to be appropriate when looking at fundamentals," he told MEPs. \par \par \uc2  "In the meantime we have witnessed a considerable improvement in the economic growth prospects for the euro area. The outlook for price stability...remains intact." \par \par \uc2  Welteke said markets had underestimated the region's potential, which he said was closing the growth gap with the U.S. in a manner that would support a euro rally. \par \par \uc2  "Non-inflationary economic growth in the euro area can overtake U.S. growth rates." \par \par \uc2  He blamed euro weakness on market doubts over European governments' chances of living up to their plans to tackle economic and structural reforms. \par \par \uc2  Earlier, his Bundesbank council colleagues Franz-Cristoph Zeitler and Hans-Juergen Koebnick both issued statements aimed at restoring confidence in the euro. \par \par \uc2  WAR OF NERVES BETWEEN ECB AND MARKETS \par \par \uc2  Prodi said he was disappointed by the euro's weakness which he attributed to the lack of a common European economic policy. \par \par \uc2  "We need an institutionalised, common economic policy which supports the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 . I am happy that the bank is independent, but it lacks a political partner with which it can hold a dialogue," Prodi said. \par \par \uc2  Separately, European Monetary Affairs Commissioner Pedro Solbes said nobody in the euro zone wanted to see a weak euro. \par \par \uc2  Speaking earlier to European Parliament deputies, Solbes said that, despite the recent spike in oil prices, the euro zone was set for a period of robust and sustained growth. \par \par \uc2  "In the future, we believe the euro should move back to a more natural rate which would reflect its historical value." \par \par \uc2  On the markets, euro sentiment remained gloomy. \par \par \uc2  "It's a war of nerves between what the ECB will do and what the major trading groups will be doing," said John Schien, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  trader at Fortis Financial Services Co. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010821dw9c08ehq\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
