\par\par \b \uc2  Options vols surge, stay bid as Aussie dlr eyes low. \b0\par\par\uc2 687 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 September 2000\par 22:42\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2000 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  CHICAGO, Sept 7 (Reuters) - Implied volatility in front-end Australian dollar options jumped to one-year highs on Thursday as the spot \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  fell to a two-year low against the dollar. \par \par \uc2  The extension of premiums in options to cover downside risk also has ramped up in recent sessions for euro/dollar, cable, dollar/yen and euro/yen, \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  options traders noted. \par \par \uc2  The steep drops have fanned market apprehension over the possibility of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Reserve Bank of Australia, Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to slow the moves. \par \par \uc2  The dollar reached multiyear highs against sterling and the Swiss franc as well as another lifetime peak against the euro. \par \par \uc2  The RBA already has \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to buy Australian dollars below $0.5600, though not aggressively, traders said. \par \par \uc2  Alan Revercomb, IDEAglobal options strategist, said high nominal volatilities, spot price erosion to one-year lows and extreme inflation in the risk reversal have made the market "dangerously biased toward the downside" on the Aussie dollar. \par \par \uc2  One-month Aussie dollar volatility jumped to a mid of about 14.20 percent, about as high as it has been this year. The risk reversal reached 1.50 in favour of puts, a 12-to 18-month high. \par \par \uc2  There was some short covering Thursday with the RBA stepping in, but the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was not too aggressive and "there may be one more test of their resolve," said Tom Rogers, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  analyst at Thomson/IFR. "There has been no panic buying since the Aussie hit the lows." \par \par \uc2  One-month Aussie volatility has jumped about 3.0 percentage points in two weeks to 14.20 percent with the spot \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  test toward $0.5530, Rogers said. \par \par \uc2  A number of stops have been lined up near $0.5525, which could be a "danger level," but the Reserve Bank might try to protect the $0.5480 area more, Rogers said. \par \par \uc2  Revercomb said hedge funds were seen buying intermediate-term low-delta Aussie calls out to next spring, reflecting some expectations for the erosion to end, at least for a time. Aussie dollar options are not terribly liquid and with the skew moving so rapidly, it becomes more difficult to trade, he added. \par \par \uc2  "It shows some panic and suggests that the market might be too far stretched to one extreme," Revercomb said. "But that doesn't necessarily mean (the decline) has to be near an end." \par \par \uc2  The Reserve Bank of Australia has been known to counter sharp \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  movements in the options market as well, he added. \par \par \uc2  Dollar/yen volatility jumped with the spot break of 105. A number of barrier options were violated from 105.50 to 105 spot, which generated demand for replacements. \par \par \uc2  Dollar/yen breaks are expected to generate more volatility than gains, so there is potential for much higher volatility if the spot falls significantly below 105, Revercomb said. \par \par \uc2  "The risk reversal is high, but nominal volatility is mild compared to where it can get when things really heat up," Revercomb added. \par \par \uc2  The threat of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  always appears more ominous from the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  than from the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  and has kept implied volatility somewhat contained, Revercomb said. \par \par \uc2  The drop below 105 in dollar/yen brings a more serious threat of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  which would create friction to slow the descent though not necessarily cause it to stop, Revercomb said. \par \par \uc2  There was a sizable purchase of dollar/yen puts at 100 spot and placement of knockouts at 110. The one-month risk reversal reached a mid of 3.0 favouring yen calls during the session and was quoted at 2.75 late Thursday afternoon. \par \par \uc2  Overnight euro/dollar volatility "exploded" on the probe to a fresh low below 86.50 cents and the one-month risk reversal firmed to 0.95 favouring euro puts. One-month volatility has stayed firm, even with the euro bounce back above 87.00 cents. \par \par \uc2  The market is now focused on this week's meeting of European Union finance ministers and signs of whether the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will try to slow the euro's fall. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010821dw9708x12\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
